('intervention', 1, 'Module 1: The Intervention Mindset',
  'What separates an intervention from a policy, a scheme, or a project; the design pipeline from problem to institutionalisation; and why well-meaning programmes so often fail.',
  $html$<h2>Module 1: The Intervention Mindset</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">Development practice is crowded with things that <em>sound</em> like solutions &mdash; a scheme, a policy, a campaign, a pilot. This course is about a narrower and harder object: the <strong>intervention</strong>, a deliberately designed package of activities meant to change a specific outcome for specific people, through a mechanism you can name. Getting the object right is the first design decision, because most failures are not failures of effort or funding. They are failures of design that were baked in before anyone reached the field.</p></div>

<h3>Policy, Scheme, Project, Intervention &mdash; Say Which You Mean</h3>
<p>These words are used interchangeably in proposals and they should not be. A <em>policy</em> is a governing rule or allocation &mdash; "every child aged 6&ndash;14 has a right to free schooling." A <em>scheme</em> is a standing government programme with a budget line. A <em>project</em> is a time-bound bundle of funded activities. An <em>intervention</em> is the causal heart inside any of these: the specific thing you do, to specific people, expected to work through a specific mechanism.</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Crosshair_detailed.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Four objects, four questions</div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Policy</div><div class="definition-text">Asks <em>what should be true</em>. The Right to Education Act declares an entitlement. It does not, by itself, teach a single child to read.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Scheme</div><div class="definition-text">Asks <em>what is funded and administered</em>. Samagra Shiksha is the delivery vehicle &mdash; budget, staff, guidelines.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Project</div><div class="definition-text">Asks <em>what we will do, by when</em>. A three-year district literacy project with a grant and a logframe.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Intervention</div><div class="definition-text">Asks <em>what change, in whom, through what mechanism</em>. Grouping children by learning level rather than grade so instruction meets them where they are. This is the design object.</div></div></div>
<p>The discipline this course keeps is to force every proposal down to its intervention: strip away the policy language and the budget lines until you can state, in one sentence, <em>who</em> is changed, <em>how</em>, and <em>why that mechanism should work</em>. If you cannot, there is no design yet &mdash; only an intention.</p>

<h3>The Design Pipeline</h3>
<p>An intervention travels a pipeline, and design decisions at each stage constrain every stage that follows. The whole course is organised around it.</p>
<div class="comparison-cards">
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon">1</span><span class="comparison-title">Diagnose</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p>Understand the problem before choosing a solution. What kind of problem is this &mdash; information, incentive, capacity, belief? (Module 2)</p></div></div>
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon">2</span><span class="comparison-title">Theorise</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p>Write a falsifiable theory of change: the mechanism and the assumptions it rides on. (Module 3)</p></div></div>
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon">3</span><span class="comparison-title">Design</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p>Choose and adapt a model &mdash; cash, a bundle, a frontline cadre, a collective, a service redesign. (Modules 4&ndash;10)</p></div></div>
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon">4</span><span class="comparison-title">Pilot</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p>Test at small scale, honestly. Learn whether the mechanism fires and whether delivery holds.</p></div></div>
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon">5</span><span class="comparison-title">Scale</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p>The hard part. Most of what works in a pilot loses its voltage on the way up. (Modules 11&ndash;13)</p></div></div>
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon">6</span><span class="comparison-title">Institutionalise</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p>Hand the intervention to a system that will run it after the designers leave. Survival, not launch, is the test.</p></div></div>
</div>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Spark.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> The designer's leverage is front-loaded</div><p>Each stage narrows the options of the next. A weak diagnosis cannot be rescued by a clever pilot; an intervention that ignores the delivery system cannot be rescued at scale. <strong>The cheapest place to fix a design is at the diagnosis; the most expensive is after national rollout.</strong> This is why the course spends its first three modules before naming a single model.</p></div>

<h3>Why Good Intentions Fail</h3>
<p>Well-funded, well-meant interventions fail in patterned ways. Naming the patterns is the beginning of designing against them.</p>
<div class="table-wrapper"><table><thead><tr><th>Failure mode</th><th>What it looks like</th><th>Where it is designed in</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Solution in search of a problem</td><td>The tool (an app, a training, a kit) is chosen first; the problem is reverse-engineered to fit it.</td><td>Diagnosis (Module 2)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Isomorphic mimicry</td><td>The intervention copies the <em>form</em> of a successful model without its <em>function</em> &mdash; committees that never meet, plans no one uses.</td><td>Design (Modules 2, 13)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Broken delivery chain</td><td>The design is sound but leaks at some link &mdash; funds delayed, staff absent, last mile unreached.</td><td>Delivery (Module 9)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pilot-to-scale collapse</td><td>It worked with a hand-picked team and motivated participants; it evaporates when multiplied.</td><td>Scaling (Modules 11&ndash;12)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Elite capture</td><td>Benefits flow to the already-powerful; the intended group is crowded out.</td><td>Targeting (Modules 7, 10)</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<div class="worked-example"><div class="worked-example-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Clipboard.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Case &mdash; two responses to the same problem</div>
<p>The problem: primary-school children in a district can attend for five years and still not read a simple paragraph &mdash; the "schooling is not learning" gap documented across South Asia (ASER, Pratham).</p>
<p><strong>Response A &mdash; more of the same.</strong> Build more classrooms, distribute more textbooks, add contract teachers. Inputs rise; the reading gap barely moves, because the binding constraint was never the number of inputs. This is a solution chosen before the problem was diagnosed.</p>
<p><strong>Response B &mdash; redesign the mechanism.</strong> Pratham's Teaching at the Right Level regroups children by what they can actually do, not by grade, for a focused daily block. The same teachers and the same hours produce large, replicated reading gains. The intervention changed the <em>mechanism</em> of instruction, not the volume of inputs.</p>
<p>Same problem, same sector, opposite outcomes &mdash; and the difference was entirely a design decision made before implementation. Module 8 returns to this case in full.</p></div>

<h3>Three Actors, Three Design Logics</h3>
<p>Interventions are delivered by the state, by markets, or by civil society, and each carries a different design logic &mdash; different reach, different incentives, different failure modes. A designer's first structural choice is <em>who delivers</em>, because it silently sets what is possible.</p>
<div class="callout callout-blue"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Info.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>State</strong> reaches everyone and can compel and fund at scale, but moves at the speed of bureaucracy and is bound by capacity. <strong>Markets</strong> are efficient and self-scaling where people can pay, and blind where they cannot. <strong>Civil society</strong> is trusted, close to the last mile, and inventive, but rarely reaches population scale on its own. The most durable interventions in this course &mdash; graduation, SHGs, ASHA &mdash; are hybrids that route each task to the actor best suited to it.</p></div></div>

<div class="reflection-prompt"><p><strong>Reflect.</strong> Take one programme you know well. Strip away its policy language and budget until you can state its <em>intervention</em> in a single sentence: who is changed, how, and through what mechanism. Now place it on the design pipeline &mdash; which stage was strongest, and which failure mode from the table is it most exposed to?</p></div>

<blockquote>"The first question is not 'what should we do?' but 'what exactly is the problem, and why has it not already been solved?'"<cite>&mdash; after Lant Pritchett &amp; Michael Woolcock</cite></blockquote>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Test your grasp of the intervention mindset before moving to diagnosis in Module 2.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> Which statement best describes an <em>intervention</em>, as distinct from a policy or scheme? <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m1q1" id="m1q1a" value="a"><label for="m1q1a">A legal entitlement that declares what should be true.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m1q1" id="m1q1b" value="b"><label for="m1q1b">A standing government programme with a budget line.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m1q1" id="m1q1c" value="c"><label for="m1q1c">A designed package of activities meant to change a specific outcome for specific people through a nameable mechanism.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m1q1" id="m1q1d" value="d"><label for="m1q1d">Any time-bound project funded by a grant.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> A district responds to a learning crisis by building more classrooms and distributing more textbooks, yet reading levels barely move. The most likely design error is: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m1q2" id="m1q2a" value="a"><label for="m1q2a">Too little funding was allocated to the inputs.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m1q2" id="m1q2b" value="b"><label for="m1q2b">A solution (more inputs) was chosen before diagnosing the binding constraint.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m1q2" id="m1q2c" value="c"><label for="m1q2c">The intervention was scaled too slowly.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m1q2" id="m1q2d" value="d"><label for="m1q2d">Textbooks are never useful in education.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> "Isomorphic mimicry" as a failure mode means that an intervention: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m1q3" id="m1q3a" value="a"><label for="m1q3a">Copies the visible form of a successful model without reproducing its function.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m1q3" id="m1q3b" value="b"><label for="m1q3b">Is delivered by the wrong actor (state instead of market).</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m1q3" id="m1q3c" value="c"><label for="m1q3c">Reaches the elite instead of the intended group.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m1q3" id="m1q3d" value="d"><label for="m1q3d">Works in a pilot but fails at scale.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Take one intervention you have worked on. Which of the five failure modes in the table was it most exposed to, and at which stage of the design pipeline was that exposure created?</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  true),
('intervention', 2, 'Module 2: Diagnosing the Problem',
  'Problem-driven versus solution-driven design, root-cause and binding-constraint thinking, and the behavioural diagnosis that decides what kind of intervention a problem actually needs.',
  $html$<h2>Module 2: Diagnosing the Problem</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">Most interventions are answers. Diagnosis is the discipline of first getting the <em>question</em> right &mdash; of naming, precisely, what is broken and why it has not already fixed itself. The costliest error in development design is not a bad solution; it is a good solution aimed at the wrong problem. A borewell for a village whose water crisis is one of distribution, not supply, is faithfully drilled, correctly maintained, and useless. This module builds the diagnostic habits that decide whether everything downstream is aimed true.</p></div>

<h3>Problem-Driven, Not Solution-Driven</h3>
<p>Organisations accumulate solutions &mdash; a training curriculum, an app, a self-help-group model, a conditional transfer &mdash; and then go looking for problems those solutions fit. This is the <em>solution in search of a problem</em> trap, and it is seductive precisely because the solution already works somewhere. Problem-driven design reverses the arrow: it begins with a sharply specified problem and lets the diagnosis dictate the class of solution, even when the answer is one the organisation does not sell.</p>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>The tell.</strong> If your team can name the intervention before it can state the problem in one falsifiable sentence, you are solution-driven. The test Pritchett, Samji and Hausmann call <em>problem-driven iterative adaptation</em> (Andrews, Pritchett &amp; Woolcock 2013) starts the other way round: name the problem, make it locally felt, and let the solution emerge from iteration rather than from the catalogue.</p></div></div>

<h3>Root-Cause Tools &mdash; and Their Limits</h3>
<p>The standard diagnostic toolkit &mdash; the <em>problem tree</em>, the <em>&ldquo;5 whys&rdquo;</em> &mdash; is useful for one thing: forcing a team past the first, shallow explanation. Ask why children are malnourished, and &ldquo;not enough food&rdquo; gives way, five whys down, to a mother&rsquo;s time poverty or a caste-segregated ration shop. But these tools have a structural weakness worth naming.</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Search.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Two tools, one shared flaw</div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Problem tree</div><div class="definition-text">Maps a core problem into its causes (roots) and effects (branches). Good for shared understanding; weak at telling you <em>which</em> root is binding &mdash; it treats every cause as co-equal.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">5 whys</div><div class="definition-text">Chains &ldquo;why?&rdquo; to drill from symptom to cause. Good for depth; but a single chain follows <em>one</em> path and quietly ignores the others.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">The shared flaw</div><div class="definition-text">Both can be run <em>backwards</em> from a pre-chosen answer &mdash; a team that wants to build toilets will find, five whys down, a tree whose deepest root is conveniently &ldquo;no toilets.&rdquo; The method rationalises the conclusion instead of testing it.</div></div></div>
<p>A root-cause exercise is only as honest as the team running it. Used to explore, it opens the problem; used to justify, it launders a decision already taken. The corrective is to force a ranking: not <em>what are the causes</em>, but <em>which single cause, relaxed, would move the outcome most</em>.</p>

<h3>Binding-Constraints Thinking</h3>
<p>That ranking question is the heart of <em>growth diagnostics</em>, developed to explain why economies stall (Hausmann, Rodrik &amp; Velasco 2005). Its logic transfers cleanly to social problems. At any moment, many things are wrong, but they are not equally wrong: one constraint <em>binds</em>, and relaxing it moves the outcome far more than fixing the others. Pour resources into a non-binding constraint and you get almost nothing &mdash; more textbooks where the constraint is instruction, more clinics where the constraint is trust.</p>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Target.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Find the one constraint that moves the outcome</div><p>The diagnostic question is not &ldquo;what are all the barriers?&rdquo; &mdash; there are always many &mdash; but &ldquo;<strong>which barrier, if removed, releases the largest change per rupee?</strong>&rdquo; The signature of a binding constraint: actors are visibly working around it, its shadow price is high, and small relaxations produce outsized responses. A well-designed intervention attacks that constraint and, deliberately, ignores the rest.</p></div>

<h3>Isomorphic Mimicry and Premature Load-Bearing</h3>
<p>A distinct diagnostic failure is to copy the <em>form</em> of something that works while missing its <em>function</em> &mdash; what Andrews, Pritchett and Woolcock (2017) call <strong>isomorphic mimicry</strong>, after organisms that evolve the appearance of a stronger species. A ministry adopts the anti-corruption commission, the results framework, the grievance portal &mdash; the visible signatures of a capable state &mdash; while the underlying function they signal never takes root. The form earns legitimacy; the function is absent.</p>
<div class="callout callout-red"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Premature load-bearing.</strong> The related trap is asking a borrowed form to carry weight its function cannot yet bear &mdash; devolving budgets to village committees that have never managed money, or mandating outcome-based contracts where no one can measure outcomes. The wall looks finished, so real load is placed on it, and it fails under exactly the pressure it was built to advertise it could take.</p></div></div>

<h3>Behavioural Diagnosis: What <em>Kind</em> of Problem Is It?</h3>
<p>Before choosing an instrument, classify the problem by the behaviour it turns on &mdash; because each type demands a different <em>class</em> of intervention, and matching the wrong class is the diagnostic error that dooms the design.</p>
<div class="table-wrapper"><table><thead><tr><th>Problem type</th><th>The person&hellip;</th><th>Intervention class it implies</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Information</td><td>would act, but does not know the fact, the benefit, or the way.</td><td>Disclosure, reminders, awareness, simplification.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Incentive</td><td>knows, but the payoff to acting is too small, too slow, or too risky.</td><td>Transfers, subsidies, pricing, insurance, penalties.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Capacity</td><td>knows and wants to, but lacks the skill, tool, time, or infrastructure.</td><td>Training, provision, access, service redesign.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Belief / norm</td><td>could act, but the surrounding social norm or belief makes the act costly or unthinkable.</td><td>Norm-shifting, community mobilisation, role models, collective commitment.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="callout callout-blue"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Info.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p>The same surface problem can be any of these. Low immunisation might be an <em>information</em> problem (parents unaware of the schedule), an <em>incentive</em> problem (the clinic is a half-day&rsquo;s lost wages away &mdash; solved by Banerjee et al.&rsquo;s lentil incentives in Rajasthan), a <em>capacity</em> problem (no cold chain), or a <em>norm</em> problem (rumour and distrust). Only the diagnosis tells you which lever to pull; guess wrong and the intervention is correct for a problem you do not have.</p></div></div>

<div class="worked-example"><div class="worked-example-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Layers.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Case &mdash; open defecation and Swachh Bharat</div>
<p>India&rsquo;s sanitation problem was long diagnosed as an <em>access</em> problem &mdash; people defecate in the open because they have no toilet &mdash; and the answer followed automatically: build toilets. Earlier campaigns and, at first, the Swachh Bharat Mission poured subsidies into latrine construction and counted units built.</p>
<p>But the binding constraint, research showed, was rarely access. Households with functioning latrines still chose open defecation, driven by beliefs about purity, pollution and caste, and a norm that open defecation was healthier or more manly (Coffey &amp; Spears 2017). This was a <strong>belief/norm problem wearing the mask of a capacity problem.</strong> A toilet is the right instrument for a capacity constraint and the wrong one for a norm constraint &mdash; so latrines were built and then used to store grain.</p>
<p><strong>The contrast.</strong> <em>Community-Led Total Sanitation</em> (CLTS) diagnosed the problem as a norm and attacked it as one &mdash; no subsidy, no construction target, but a facilitated collective &ldquo;triggering&rdquo; of disgust and shared commitment that shifted the norm village by village. Swachh Bharat&rsquo;s later, more effective phase absorbed exactly this lesson, pairing construction with intensive behaviour-change communication. Same sector, same outcome sought &mdash; the diagnosis, not the toilet, determined whether the design could work.</p></div>

<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Spark.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Diagnosis before design</div><p>The intervention class is not a choice made after the problem is understood &mdash; it is <em>implied</em> by how the problem is understood. Misdiagnose the type and no amount of implementation quality can save you: a norm problem answered with hardware, an incentive problem answered with a poster, a capacity problem answered with a rule. <strong>Get the diagnosis right and the design half-writes itself; get it wrong and every later module is compounding a first-order error.</strong></p></div>

<div class="reflection-prompt"><p><strong>Reflect.</strong> Take a problem your organisation is working on. Run the classification: is it, at its binding constraint, an information, incentive, capacity, or belief/norm problem &mdash; and be honest about whether your current intervention matches that type or the type you wish it were. Then ask the harder question: could you have run a problem tree <em>backwards</em> from the solution you already had?</p></div>

<blockquote>&ldquo;A problem well stated is a problem half solved &mdash; and in development, most problems are never stated at all, only assumed.&rdquo;<cite>&mdash; after Charles Kettering, applied to development design</cite></blockquote>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Test your grasp of problem diagnosis before moving to theories of change in Module 3.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> A team can name the intervention it will run before it can state the problem in one falsifiable sentence. This is the clearest sign of: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m2q1" id="m2q1a" value="a"><label for="m2q1a">A well-run problem tree.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m2q1" id="m2q1b" value="b"><label for="m2q1b">Correct binding-constraint analysis.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m2q1" id="m2q1c" value="c"><label for="m2q1c">Solution-driven design &mdash; a solution in search of a problem.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m2q1" id="m2q1d" value="d"><label for="m2q1d">Behavioural diagnosis done correctly.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> Households in a district have functioning latrines but many still defecate in the open. Applying behavioural diagnosis, the binding constraint is best classified as: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m2q2" id="m2q2a" value="a"><label for="m2q2a">A capacity problem &mdash; solved by building more toilets.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m2q2" id="m2q2b" value="b"><label for="m2q2b">A belief/norm problem &mdash; requiring norm-shifting, not hardware.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m2q2" id="m2q2c" value="c"><label for="m2q2c">An information problem &mdash; solved by a single awareness poster.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m2q2" id="m2q2d" value="d"><label for="m2q2d">An incentive problem &mdash; solved by a construction subsidy.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> Adapting Hausmann&ndash;Rodrik growth-diagnostics logic to a social problem, the key diagnostic question is: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m2q3" id="m2q3a" value="a"><label for="m2q3a">Which single constraint, if relaxed, would move the outcome most per rupee?</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m2q3" id="m2q3b" value="b"><label for="m2q3b">What is the full list of every barrier, treating each as equally important?</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m2q3" id="m2q3c" value="c"><label for="m2q3c">Which successful model from another country can we copy the form of?</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m2q3" id="m2q3d" value="d"><label for="m2q3d">How quickly can we scale the solution we have already chosen?</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Take a problem your team is working on. Classify it as an information, incentive, capacity, or belief/norm problem &mdash; then ask whether your current intervention matches that type, or whether you ran the diagnosis backwards from a solution you had already chosen.</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('intervention', 3, 'Module 3: Theory of Change as a Design Instrument',
  'The theory of change not as a compliance artifact but as a falsifiable design hypothesis, with its assumptions made explicit, ranked, and testable before a rupee is spent.',
  $html$<h2>Module 3: Theory of Change as a Design Instrument</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">A theory of change is the most abused document in development. In most files it is a diagram drawn <em>after</em> the design was fixed, to satisfy a donor template &mdash; a decorative box-and-arrow chart no one revisits. This module treats it as something far more useful and far more demanding: a <strong>falsifiable hypothesis about how change happens</strong>, the spine that holds an intervention upright. A good theory of change can be wrong, and knowing exactly how it could be wrong is what turns it from paperwork into a design instrument.</p></div>
<h3>The Theory of Change Is a Hypothesis, Not a Diagram</h3>
<p>A theory of change (ToC) states the causal claim inside your intervention: <em>if</em> we do these activities, <em>then</em> these outputs will trigger this mechanism, <em>which will</em> produce this outcome, <em>because</em> a set of assumptions holds. The word that matters is <em>because</em>. Carol Weiss, who gave the field the term, insisted that a theory of change is valuable precisely when it is specific enough to be proven wrong (Weiss 1995). A claim that cannot fail cannot guide design.</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Crosshair_detailed.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> The anatomy of a theory of change</div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Inputs &amp; activities</div><div class="definition-text">What you fund and do &mdash; train, transfer, build, convene. The part organisations control directly.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Outputs</div><div class="definition-text">The immediate, countable products of activity &mdash; women trained, kits delivered, meetings held. Necessary, but not yet change.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Mechanism</div><div class="definition-text">The behavioural or systemic reason an output <em>becomes</em> an outcome &mdash; the causal engine. This is the part most theories leave blank.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Outcome &amp; assumptions</div><div class="definition-text">The change you are actually after, and the conditions that must hold for the mechanism to fire. The assumptions are where the theory is most likely to break.</div></div></div>
<p>Andrea Vogel's review of the concept for DFID found that the term had drifted to mean almost anything &mdash; a results chain, a narrative, a workshop output &mdash; and that its power was recoverable only when practitioners wrote the assumptions and the causal pathway explicitly, not just the boxes (Vogel 2012). The rest of this module is about doing exactly that.</p>
<h3>Assumptions Are the Weakest Link</h3>
<p>Every arrow in a theory of change hides an assumption &mdash; a condition that must be true for the arrow to hold. Interventions rarely fail because the activities were not done; they fail because a load-bearing assumption quietly did not hold. The designer&rsquo;s job is not to list every assumption but to find the <em>killer</em> one: the assumption that is both uncertain and consequential, the single point where the whole theory is most likely to snap.</p>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p>Rank assumptions on two axes: <strong>uncertainty</strong> (how confident are we this holds?) and <strong>consequence</strong> (if it fails, does the intervention still work?). The assumptions in the high-uncertainty, high-consequence corner are your <em>killer assumptions</em>. Design a cheap test for each one <em>before</em> committing the budget &mdash; that test is often worth more than the pilot itself.</p></div></div>
<div class="table-wrapper"><table><thead><tr><th>Assumption (SHG livelihoods programme)</th><th>Uncertainty</th><th>Consequence if false</th><th>Priority</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Trained women can sell their produce at a viable price (market access)</td><td>High</td><td>Fatal &mdash; income never rises</td><td><strong>Test first</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>Women can attend training given care and farm work</td><td>Medium</td><td>High &mdash; low take-up</td><td>Monitor closely</td></tr>
<tr><td>The trainer&rsquo;s skills transfer to the local crop and soil</td><td>Medium</td><td>Medium &mdash; weaker gains</td><td>Adapt content</td></tr>
<tr><td>Group members will not free-ride on the collective</td><td>Low</td><td>Medium &mdash; managed by norms</td><td>Watch</td></tr>
<tr><td>Banks will disburse the linked credit on time</td><td>Medium</td><td>High &mdash; delays stall activity</td><td>Build buffer</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h3>Design for the Mechanism, Not the Activity</h3>
<p>The most common flaw in a theory of change is to mistake the activity for the mechanism &mdash; to assume that doing the thing <em>is</em> the change. "We trained 500 women" is an output. Whether training changes anything depends on a mechanism: does new knowledge alter what women actually do, and does that altered behaviour survive contact with markets, households, and time? Activity thinking counts what you deliver; mechanism thinking asks <em>why the delivery should move the outcome at all</em>.</p>
<div class="comparison-cards">
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Settings.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></span><span class="comparison-title">Activity thinking</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p>Success = activities completed.</p><p>"We held 40 trainings and delivered 500 toolkits."</p><p>Assumes output automatically becomes outcome.</p><p>Reports what was done; silent on why it should work.</p><p>Fails invisibly &mdash; targets met, outcome flat.</p></div></div>
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Spark.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></span><span class="comparison-title">Mechanism thinking</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p>Success = the causal engine fires.</p><p>"Training raises confidence and skill, so women adopt a higher-value crop and sell it."</p><p>Names the behaviour that turns output into outcome.</p><p>States what must be true for the arrow to hold.</p><p>Fails loudly &mdash; you can see which link broke.</p></div></div>
</div>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Layers.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Results chains and the dose-response question</div><p>An outcome is rarely produced by one activity; it is produced by a <em>causal package</em> &mdash; a chain of linked outputs and behaviours that must all hold together. Once you have named the chain, ask the question activity thinking never asks: <strong>is the dose enough?</strong> Three days of training, a one-time transfer, a single visit &mdash; each is a <em>dose</em>, and a dose too small to move the outcome is not a smaller version of success but a designed-in failure. Graduation programmes work partly because they deliver a large, bundled, sustained dose; a thin version of the same package moves nothing.</p></div>
<h3>From Theory of Change to Logframe &mdash; Without Losing the Theory</h3>
<p>The logical framework (Logframe 101) is the donor&rsquo;s reporting grid: objectives, indicators, means of verification, assumptions, arranged in rows. It is useful for accountability and dangerous for design, because its tidy rows <em>flatten the causal logic</em> &mdash; the mechanism disappears into an "output" cell, and the assumptions get demoted to a narrow final column no one reads. A theory of change is written to be argued with; a logframe is written to be audited against. Build the theory of change first, then compress it into the logframe &mdash; never the reverse.</p>
<div class="callout callout-red"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>The flattening risk.</strong> When a logframe is written first, the mechanism is never stated, so the intervention is managed by counting outputs. Staff hit every target in the grid while the outcome stays flat &mdash; and because the grid has no row for "why this should work," no one can see where the theory broke. Keep the theory of change as the living document; let the logframe be its compressed shadow, not its replacement.</p></div></div>
<div class="worked-example"><div class="worked-example-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Clipboard.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Case &mdash; a theory of change for an SHG livelihoods programme</div>
<p>The design: organise poor rural women into self-help groups, train them in a higher-value activity (say, vegetable cultivation or dairy), link them to credit, and expect household income to rise &mdash; the logic behind large programmes like India&rsquo;s NRLM.</p>
<p><strong>The tidy theory.</strong> Form groups &rarr; train women &rarr; women adopt the new activity &rarr; production rises &rarr; income rises &rarr; wellbeing improves. Every arrow looks obvious, which is exactly why the theory is dangerous: obviousness hides the assumptions.</p>
<p><strong>The killer assumption.</strong> Surface the assumptions behind each arrow and one stands out &mdash; between "production rises" and "income rises" sits the silent claim that <em>trained women will find a market willing to buy their produce at a viable price</em>. It is highly uncertain (local markets may be thin, saturated, or controlled by intermediaries) and fatal if false (a shed full of unsold vegetables is not income). This is the market-access assumption, and it is the load-bearing wall of the whole design.</p>
<p><strong>How testing it early changes the design.</strong> Instead of training 500 women and discovering the market problem at the end, run a cheap early test: place a small first cohort&rsquo;s produce and watch what happens to price and offtake. If the market clears, scale the training. If it does not, the design changes <em>before</em> the money is spent &mdash; add an aggregation and buyer-linkage component, sequence production to demand, or choose a different activity entirely. Testing the killer assumption first is worth more than a perfectly executed programme built on a false one.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-blue"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Layers.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Sibling reference &mdash; the counterfactual spine.</strong> The MEL flagship treats the same causal claim from the measurement side: a theory of change tells you <em>what</em> to measure and <em>where</em> the causal link is that a counterfactual must isolate. Your theory of change names the mechanism; the counterfactual spine tests whether that mechanism, and not something else, produced the change. Design and evaluation are two readings of one hypothesis &mdash; write the theory of change so the evaluator can find the link you are claiming.</p></div></div>
<div class="reflection-prompt"><p><strong>Reflect.</strong> Take one intervention you know and write its theory of change as an <em>if&hellip;then&hellip;because</em> sentence, not a diagram. Now list the assumptions hiding in each arrow and rank them by uncertainty &times; consequence. Which is the killer assumption &mdash; and what is the cheapest test you could run <em>before</em> committing the budget?</p></div>
<blockquote>"A theory of change is only useful if it is specific enough to be wrong. The point is not to defend it, but to find out where it breaks &mdash; while the change is still cheap to make."<cite>&mdash; after Carol Weiss (1995) &amp; Isabel Vogel (2012)</cite></blockquote>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Test your grasp of theory of change as a design instrument before moving to choosing a model in Module 4.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> What makes a theory of change useful as a <em>design instrument</em> rather than a donor compliance artifact? <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m3q1" id="m3q1a" value="a"><label for="m3q1a">It is drawn as a clear box-and-arrow diagram after the design is fixed.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m3q1" id="m3q1b" value="b"><label for="m3q1b">It lists every activity and output the programme will deliver.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m3q1" id="m3q1c" value="c"><label for="m3q1c">It is a falsifiable hypothesis &mdash; specific enough that you can say exactly how it could be proven wrong.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m3q1" id="m3q1d" value="d"><label for="m3q1d">It satisfies the donor&rsquo;s reporting template.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> In an SHG livelihoods programme, which assumption should be tested <em>first</em>, and why? <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m3q2" id="m3q2a" value="a"><label for="m3q2a">That group members will not free-ride &mdash; low uncertainty, medium consequence.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m3q2" id="m3q2b" value="b"><label for="m3q2b">That trained women can sell their produce at a viable price &mdash; high uncertainty and fatal if false.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m3q2" id="m3q2c" value="c"><label for="m3q2c">That the trainer&rsquo;s skills transfer to the local crop &mdash; it is the cheapest to fix.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m3q2" id="m3q2d" value="d"><label for="m3q2d">That banks disburse credit on time &mdash; it is the only assumption that matters.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> A programme reports "500 women trained and 500 toolkits delivered," yet household income is flat. Which design distinction best explains the gap? <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m3q3" id="m3q3a" value="a"><label for="m3q3a">The programme counted activities and outputs but never designed for the mechanism that turns output into outcome.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m3q3" id="m3q3b" value="b"><label for="m3q3b">The logframe had too many indicators.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m3q3" id="m3q3c" value="c"><label for="m3q3c">The theory of change diagram was not colourful enough.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m3q3" id="m3q3d" value="d"><label for="m3q3d">Too few women were trained; the dose should have been larger.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Take an intervention you have worked on and write its theory of change as an <em>if&hellip;then&hellip;because</em> sentence. Which assumption is its killer assumption &mdash; high on both uncertainty and consequence &mdash; and what cheap test could have surfaced it before the budget was committed?</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('intervention', 4, 'Module 4: Cash — Transfers, Targeting & Delivery Rails',
  'Conditional and unconditional cash transfers, how targeting is chosen and what it costs, and the delivery rails — DBT, Aadhaar, JAM — that decide whether the money actually arrives.',
  $html$<h2>Module 4: Cash &mdash; Transfers, Targeting &amp; Delivery Rails</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">Cash is the most-studied intervention in development, and the most misunderstood. Give a poor household money and you have made a design choice with a dozen downstream decisions folded into it: attach conditions or not, target the few or reach the many, and &mdash; the part designers forget &mdash; build the plumbing that actually moves rupees to a bank account in a village. This module treats cash not as charity but as engineering: the transfer, its targeting, and the delivery rails that decide whether the money arrives at all.</p></div>
<h3>Conditional Cash Transfers &mdash; The Design That Launched a Field</h3>
<p>Modern cash design begins in Mexico. In 1997, Progresa (later Oportunidades, then Prospera) paid poor mothers a stipend <em>on condition</em> that their children attended school and turned up for health check-ups. The novelty was not the money; it was that the programme was built with a randomised evaluation inside it, so the world could finally see whether cash moved outcomes. It did &mdash; enrolment, nutrition, and health use all rose &mdash; and the design was copied across Latin America and beyond (Fiszbein &amp; Schady 2009).</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Money.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> What conditionality buys, and what it costs</div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Buys</div><div class="definition-text">Political legitimacy (voters accept transfers tied to &ldquo;responsible&rdquo; behaviour), and a nudge on outcomes the state cares about &mdash; schooling, immunisation, antenatal visits &mdash; that pure income might not reach.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Costs</div><div class="definition-text">An expensive monitoring apparatus to verify compliance, and exclusion of the very poorest when they cannot meet the condition &mdash; no nearby school, no clinic, a disabled child. The condition can punish the supply-side gap it did not cause.</div></div></div>
<p>India runs its own conditional cash line. Janani Suraksha Yojana (JSY) pays a woman a cash incentive to deliver her baby in a health facility rather than at home &mdash; conditional cash aimed squarely at reducing maternal and newborn mortality. It drove a large shift toward institutional delivery, and also exposed the classic limit: paying women to reach clinics does little where the clinic is empty of staff or supplies.</p>
<h3>Unconditional Cash &mdash; &ldquo;Just Give Money&rdquo;</h3>
<p>The opposite design strips the conditions away. Unconditional cash transfers (UCTs) trust the household to know its own binding constraint. The strongest evidence comes from GiveDirectly, which sends large lump sums to poor households in East Africa and measures the result: recipients raised assets, consumption, and psychological well-being, with no rise in spending on alcohol or tobacco &mdash; the fear that reliably haunts the debate and reliably fails to appear (Haushofer &amp; Shapiro 2016).</p>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Spark.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Cash is the benchmark, not the default</div><p>Because cash is cheap to deliver and hard to misspend on the recipient&rsquo;s behalf, it has become the yardstick every in-kind programme must now beat. The honest design question is no longer &ldquo;does cash work?&rdquo; but <strong>&ldquo;does your training, kit, or service beat simply handing people the money it costs?&rdquo;</strong> Often the in-kind programme cannot &mdash; and that comparison is itself a design discipline.</p></div>
<h3>India&rsquo;s Rails &mdash; DBT and the JAM Trinity</h3>
<p>India&rsquo;s contribution to cash design is not the transfer but the <em>rail</em>. Direct Benefit Transfer (DBT) moves money straight into a beneficiary&rsquo;s bank account, bypassing the chain of intermediaries that historically skimmed it. It rides on the JAM trinity: <strong>J</strong>an Dhan (near-universal no-frills bank accounts), <strong>A</strong>adhaar (a unique biometric ID that de-duplicates beneficiary lists), and <strong>M</strong>obile (the channel and the confirmation). Plumbing, not policy, is the innovation.</p>
<div class="callout callout-blue"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Layers.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Delivery rails are design, not logistics.</strong> PM-KISAN deposits an income-support instalment directly to farmers&rsquo; accounts three times a year; PAHAL (DBT-LPG) pays the cooking-gas subsidy into accounts instead of discounting the cylinder at the point of sale. The same policy &mdash; &ldquo;subsidise cooking fuel&rdquo; &mdash; delivered on a different rail produces wildly different leakage. The rail is where the intervention succeeds or leaks.</p></div></div>
<div class="stat-grid">
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">&#8377;40L cr+</div><div class="stat-label">Cumulative value routed through DBT since 2013 across 300+ schemes</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">500M+</div><div class="stat-label">Jan Dhan accounts opened &mdash; the base layer of the cash rail</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">&#8377;3L cr+</div><div class="stat-label">Cumulative savings the government attributes to de-duplication and plugged leakage</div></div>
</div>
<div class="worked-example"><div class="worked-example-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Clipboard.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Case &mdash; PAHAL / DBT-LPG: how a rail cut leakage</div>
<p>Before reform, cooking-gas subsidy was delivered by selling the cylinder cheap. That in-kind discount invited two leaks: <em>ghost</em> connections (subsidised cylinders booked against people who did not exist) and <em>diversion</em> (subsidised domestic cylinders resold into the commercial market at a profit).</p>
<p><strong>The redesign.</strong> Under PAHAL, the consumer pays the full market price for the cylinder and the subsidy is transferred separately into an Aadhaar-linked bank account. A ghost cannot hold a bank account and a diverted cylinder earns no arbitrage, because everyone now pays the same market price. The rail did the work: de-duplication removed non-existent beneficiaries and the direct deposit removed the resale margin.</p>
<p><strong>The caution.</strong> The same biometric rail that removes ghosts can also remove real people &mdash; a failed fingerprint match, an un-seeded account, a migrant far from her home branch. A rail that plugs leakage by tightening the gate must be watched for <em>exclusion error</em>: the genuine poor wrongly shut out. Efficiency and inclusion pull against each other, and the designer owns both.</p></div>
<h3>Targeting &mdash; Who Gets the Money</h3>
<p>Every transfer needs a rule for who is in. There are four families of rule, and the choice is a trade-off among accuracy, administrative cost, stigma, and political durability &mdash; not a search for a single best method.</p>
<div class="table-wrapper"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Targeting accuracy</th><th>Admin cost</th><th>Stigma / take-up</th><th>Political economy</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Proxy-means test (PMT)</strong><br>Score households on observable assets to estimate poverty (India&rsquo;s SECC deprivation data).</td><td>Moderate &mdash; predicts, does not measure; errors at the margin.</td><td>High &mdash; surveys, data systems, re-verification.</td><td>Moderate &mdash; opaque score can feel arbitrary.</td><td>Fragile &mdash; excluded near-poor resent the cut-off.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Community-based</strong><br>Let villagers or the gram sabha identify the poor.</td><td>Good on local knowledge; vulnerable to elite capture.</td><td>Low &mdash; uses existing local institutions.</td><td>High &mdash; public identification can shame.</td><td>Mixed &mdash; legitimate if fair, corrosive if captured.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Self-targeting</strong><br>Design the benefit so only the needy want it (MGNREGA&rsquo;s manual work at a modest wage).</td><td>Good &mdash; the non-poor opt out by choice.</td><td>Low &mdash; no roster to build or police.</td><td>Low &mdash; claiming is a right, not a plea.</td><td>Durable &mdash; hard to attack a work-for-wage right.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Universal</strong><br>Everyone in a category is eligible (PDS in universal states, a would-be UBI).</td><td>No exclusion error by design; large inclusion of non-poor.</td><td>Low per-head but high total (no savings from screening).</td><td>None &mdash; no test, no stigma.</td><td>Very durable &mdash; broad base defends the programme.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>The two errors trade off, and they are not symmetric.</strong> Tighter targeting cuts <em>inclusion error</em> (leakage to the non-poor) but raises <em>exclusion error</em> (the genuine poor wrongly left out) &mdash; and for the poorest, being wrongly excluded is far more damaging than a rich neighbour being wrongly included. A design that optimises only for &ldquo;savings&rdquo; is quietly choosing the more harmful error.</p></div></div>
<h3>When Cash Works &mdash; and When It Cannot</h3>
<p>Cash relaxes the constraint of <em>money</em>. It does nothing for constraints that are not about money. This is the single most important boundary condition in the module.</p>
<div class="callout callout-red"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Crosshair_detailed.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Cash is not a cure for a supply-side gap.</strong> Paying a family to send its daughter to school does nothing if there is no functioning school, no teacher, no clinic within reach. Conditional cash for institutional delivery cannot manufacture a staffed labour room. Diagnose the binding constraint first (Module 2): if it is demand or liquidity, cash is powerful; if it is supply, cash can even do harm by loading demand onto a system that cannot meet it.</p></div></div>
<div class="reflection-prompt"><p><strong>Reflect.</strong> Take a cash or subsidy programme you know. State its three design choices out loud: conditional or unconditional, which targeting method, and which delivery rail. Now ask &mdash; is the constraint it addresses really about money, or is there a supply-side gap that no amount of cash will close? Which of the two targeting errors is it optimising against, and is that the error that does the most harm here?</p></div>
<blockquote>&ldquo;The poor are experts on their own lives; the design question is whether the plumbing lets the money reach them, and whether money was ever the missing thing.&rdquo;<cite>&mdash; after Fiszbein &amp; Schady, and Banerjee &amp; Duflo</cite></blockquote>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Test your grasp of cash design &mdash; conditionality, targeting, and delivery rails &mdash; before moving on.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> What did India&rsquo;s DBT reform, riding on the JAM trinity, primarily change about cash and subsidy delivery? <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m4q1" id="m4q1a" value="a"><label for="m4q1a">It made every transfer conditional on school attendance.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m4q1" id="m4q1b" value="b"><label for="m4q1b">It raised the value of each transfer without changing how it was paid.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m4q1" id="m4q1c" value="c"><label for="m4q1c">It changed the delivery rail &mdash; money moves straight to an Aadhaar-linked bank account, cutting intermediaries and de-duplicating beneficiary lists.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m4q1" id="m4q1d" value="d"><label for="m4q1d">It replaced all cash transfers with in-kind goods.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> A programme pays families a cash incentive to deliver babies in health facilities, but maternal outcomes barely improve in one district. The most likely reason is: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m4q2" id="m4q2a" value="a"><label for="m4q2a">Cash transfers never influence health behaviour.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m4q2" id="m4q2b" value="b"><label for="m4q2b">The binding constraint is on the supply side &mdash; unstaffed or unequipped facilities &mdash; which cash to households cannot fix.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m4q2" id="m4q2c" value="c"><label for="m4q2c">The transfer was unconditional rather than conditional.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m4q2" id="m4q2d" value="d"><label for="m4q2d">The families spent the money on alcohol and tobacco.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="d"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> MGNREGA offers manual work at a modest wage, so that mainly those who need the income turn up. Which targeting method does this illustrate? <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m4q3" id="m4q3a" value="a"><label for="m4q3a">Proxy-means testing.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m4q3" id="m4q3b" value="b"><label for="m4q3b">Community-based targeting.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m4q3" id="m4q3c" value="c"><label for="m4q3c">Universal eligibility.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m4q3" id="m4q3d" value="d"><label for="m4q3d">Self-targeting &mdash; the benefit is designed so the non-poor opt out by choice.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Take a cash or subsidy programme you know. Which of the four targeting methods does it use, and which of the two errors &mdash; including the non-poor or excluding the genuine poor &mdash; is its design quietly optimising against?</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('intervention', 5, 'Module 5: Bundles & Sequencing — The Graduation Approach',
  'BRAC''s ultra-poor graduation model, the six-country randomised trial that made it canonical, and the design lesson of complementarity, sequencing, and the household-level big push.',
  $html$<h2>Module 5: Bundles &amp; Sequencing &mdash; The Graduation Approach</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">Most anti-poverty interventions hand the poor a single instrument &mdash; a loan, a grant, a training, a cash transfer &mdash; and hope it is the one thing that was missing. The <strong>graduation approach</strong> begins from the opposite premise: the poorest are held by <em>several</em> constraints at once, and relieving any one alone leaves the others to pull them back. Its answer is a <strong>sequenced bundle</strong> of complementary supports, delivered in a deliberate order over about two years, designed to push a household past a poverty trap and leave it standing on its own. Pioneered by BRAC in Bangladesh, it is the clearest case in this course of a design where the <em>combination</em>, and the <em>timing</em>, are the intervention.</p></div>
<h3>The Bundle, and Why It Is Sequenced</h3>
<p>BRAC&rsquo;s Targeting the Ultra-Poor programme (launched 2002) assembles five components that are useless in isolation but powerful together. Crucially, they are not dumped on a household at once &mdash; they are layered in sequence, each preparing the ground for the next, over roughly 24 months (Bandiera et al. 2017).</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Layers.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Five layers, delivered in order</div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">1. Productive asset transfer</div><div class="definition-text">A one-time grant of a livelihood asset &mdash; typically livestock (a cow, goats, poultry). Not a loan: an outright gift that seeds an income stream the household could never have saved toward.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">2. Consumption support</div><div class="definition-text">A small, time-bound stipend for the first months, so the family need not eat or sell the asset to survive before it starts to pay. This buys the asset time to mature.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">3. Skills training</div><div class="definition-text">Practical instruction in tending the asset &mdash; animal care, husbandry, basic enterprise &mdash; so the transfer becomes a managed business, not a windfall.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">4. Coaching &amp; handholding</div><div class="definition-text">Regular home visits by a field officer over the full period: problem-solving, encouragement, and the social recognition that the household is worth investing in.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">5. Savings</div><div class="definition-text">Enrolment in a savings discipline, so that surplus is banked rather than dissipated &mdash; the mechanism by which the gains become permanent after support ends.</div></div></div>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Spark.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Order is a design variable, not an accident</div><p>Consumption support must arrive <em>with</em> the asset, or the family eats the cow. Training must land <em>before</em> the asset can be mismanaged. Coaching runs <em>throughout</em>; savings is emphasised <em>as</em> income begins. Reshuffle the sequence and the same five components fail. <strong>The graduation model treats timing as part of the design, not the delivery.</strong></p></div>
<h3>Complementarity: The Bundle Beats the Sum of Its Parts</h3>
<p>The single-instrument habit asks &ldquo;which one input works best?&rdquo; The graduation logic answers that the question is wrong: the inputs are <em>complements</em>, each raising the return to the others. An asset without training is squandered; training without an asset is theory; an asset without consumption support is sold under duress. The point is not to find the strongest ingredient but to remove several binding constraints at once so that none can drag the household back.</p>
<div class="comparison-cards">
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Crosshair_detailed.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></span><span class="comparison-title">Single-instrument thinking</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p>Cash-only, or training-only, or credit-only. Assumes one missing input. Cheap and simple, but leaves the other constraints intact &mdash; gains often fade once the transfer stops.</p></div></div>
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Layers.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></span><span class="comparison-title">Sequenced bundle</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p>Asset + consumption + skills + coaching + savings, layered over ~24 months. Costlier and heavier to run, but relieves several constraints together &mdash; gains persist after support ends.</p></div></div>
</div>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p>Complementarity cuts both ways. A bundle is only as strong as its weakest layer: drop the coaching to save money and the asset is mismanaged; drop the consumption support and it is sold. Cost-cutting a bundled intervention component by component is how a graduation programme quietly turns back into an asset giveaway.</p></div></div>
<h3>The Evidence: A Big Push That Held</h3>
<p>Graduation is one of the most rigorously tested interventions in development. Six simultaneous randomised trials &mdash; Ethiopia, Ghana, Honduras, India, Pakistan, Peru &mdash; found consistent, lasting gains a full year <em>after</em> all support ended, across consumption, assets, food security, income and psychological well-being (Banerjee, Duflo et al., <em>Science</em> 2015). The Bangladesh evaluation tracked households for seven years and showed the poorest shifting occupation out of casual labour into self-employment &mdash; a durable change in the kind of economic life available to them (Bandiera et al. 2017).</p>
<div class="stat-grid">
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">6</div><div class="stat-label">countries, one coordinated RCT (Science 2015)</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">~24 mo</div><div class="stat-label">sequenced support per household</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">1 yr+</div><div class="stat-label">gains persisted after support ended</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">7 yrs</div><div class="stat-label">tracked in Bangladesh &mdash; occupational shift held</div></div>
</div>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Arrow_upward.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> A big push, not a nudge</div><p>The design intuition is a <strong>poverty trap</strong>: below a threshold of assets and skills, a household cannot accumulate &mdash; every shock forces it to sell what little it has. A small nudge slides back. The bundle is engineered as a single large push over the threshold, after which the household&rsquo;s own savings and enterprise carry it forward. That is why the gains last: the intervention changed the household&rsquo;s <em>starting point</em>, not just its current income.</p></div>
<h3>From Lean NGO to Government Scale</h3>
<p>Graduation was built by a disciplined NGO running a tight, high-touch protocol. Its promise &mdash; and its peril &mdash; is what happens when governments adopt it. In India, the approach was absorbed into state rural livelihoods missions (SRLMs) under the National Rural Livelihoods Mission (NRLM), layering graduation onto the existing self-help-group architecture to reach the &ldquo;poorest of the poor&rdquo; that SHGs alone often miss.</p>
<div class="table-wrapper"><table><thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Lean NGO (BRAC)</th><th>Government mission (SRLM / NRLM)</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Delivery cadre</td><td>Trained, supervised field officers on a fixed protocol</td><td>Community resource persons + existing SHG staff, variable capacity</td></tr>
<tr><td>Targeting</td><td>Participatory wealth ranking, tightly verified</td><td>Layered on SECC / SHG lists &mdash; risk of inclusion and exclusion error</td></tr>
<tr><td>Coaching intensity</td><td>Frequent home visits, the costly core</td><td>First component to thin out under staff load</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fidelity pressure</td><td>Protocol owned by one organisation</td><td>Diluted across a bureaucracy with many priorities</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="callout callout-blue"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Info.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p>When a bundled intervention enters a bureaucracy, the layers most likely to erode are the invisible, labour-intensive ones &mdash; coaching and sequencing &mdash; precisely the ones that made it work. The asset transfer survives (it is procurable and countable); the handholding fades. Modules 11&ndash;12 return to this as the central scaling problem: preserving the <em>function</em> of a bundle, not just its <em>form</em>.</p></div></div>
<div class="worked-example"><div class="worked-example-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_User.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Case &mdash; graduation into an Indian SRLM</div>
<p>A state rural livelihoods mission decides to reach ultra-poor households its SHGs have never enrolled &mdash; landless, no reliable earner, outside the group system. It adopts the graduation bundle: a goat or poultry asset, a few months&rsquo; consumption stipend, husbandry training, visits from a community resource person, and enrolment in an SHG savings group.</p>
<p><strong>What travels well.</strong> The asset transfer and savings link ride on infrastructure the mission already has &mdash; procurement, bank linkage, the SHG network. Targeting the poorest into existing groups extends the mission&rsquo;s reach downward.</p>
<p><strong>What strains.</strong> Coaching intensity collapses first: one resource person covering hundreds of households cannot replicate BRAC&rsquo;s visit frequency. Sequencing slips when asset delivery and stipend arrive out of order because of procurement calendars. Where fidelity to the sequence is protected, results approach the trial gains; where it is not, the bundle decays into an asset giveaway with fading effects.</p>
<p><strong>The design lesson.</strong> Adoption by a mission is not free replication &mdash; it is a re-design problem. The question is which layers can be delegated to a bureaucracy and which must be protected as non-negotiable, a judgement Modules 11&ndash;13 make explicit.</p></div>
<div class="reflection-prompt"><p><strong>Reflect.</strong> Take a single-instrument programme you know &mdash; a cash transfer, a training, a microloan. What are the <em>other</em> binding constraints it leaves untouched, and which two or three complementary components would you sequence around it to turn a nudge into a push? Then ask the harder question: which of those components would a government system quietly drop first?</p></div>
<blockquote>&ldquo;The very poor face not one trap but several, knotted together; you cannot cut one strand and expect the knot to fall open.&rdquo;<cite>&mdash; after Banerjee &amp; Duflo, <em>Poor Economics</em></cite></blockquote>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Test your grasp of bundles and sequencing before moving on.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> What makes the graduation approach a <em>sequenced bundle</em> rather than a set of separate benefits? <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m5q1" id="m5q1a" value="a"><label for="m5q1a">It gives households the largest possible cash grant in one payment.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m5q1" id="m5q1b" value="b"><label for="m5q1b">It lets each household choose which single component it prefers.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m5q1" id="m5q1c" value="c"><label for="m5q1c">Its five components are complementary and layered in a deliberate order over ~24 months, so each prepares the ground for the next.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m5q1" id="m5q1d" value="d"><label for="m5q1d">It replaces training with coaching so no skills instruction is needed.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> The six-country randomised trial (Banerjee, Duflo et al., <em>Science</em> 2015) is most notable because the gains: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m5q2" id="m5q2a" value="a"><label for="m5q2a">Appeared only during the period that support was being delivered.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m5q2" id="m5q2b" value="b"><label for="m5q2b">Persisted across multiple outcomes a year after all support had ended, in six different countries.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m5q2" id="m5q2c" value="c"><label for="m5q2c">Were found in Bangladesh but nowhere else.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m5q2" id="m5q2d" value="d"><label for="m5q2d">Came entirely from the cash consumption stipend, not the asset.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> When graduation is adopted by an Indian state rural livelihoods mission (SRLM/NRLM), which component is most likely to erode first? <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m5q3" id="m5q3a" value="a"><label for="m5q3a">The intensive coaching and handholding, because it is labour-intensive and hard to staff at scale.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m5q3" id="m5q3b" value="b"><label for="m5q3b">The productive asset transfer, because assets are hard to procure through government systems.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m5q3" id="m5q3c" value="c"><label for="m5q3c">The savings link, because SHGs have no banking infrastructure.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m5q3" id="m5q3d" value="d"><label for="m5q3d">Nothing erodes; a bureaucracy replicates the NGO protocol exactly.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Pick one component of the graduation bundle and trace what happens to the other four if it is dropped to save cost. Which single omission would do the most damage to the household &mdash; and why does that reveal the bundle as a design, not a menu?</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('intervention', 6, 'Module 6: Human Infrastructure — Frontline Workers & the Last Mile',
  'Community health workers — ASHA and BRAC''s shasthya shebika — incentive design for frontline agents, and the delivery-chain view of selection, training, supervision, and attrition.',
  $html$<h2>Module 6: Human Infrastructure &mdash; Frontline Workers &amp; the Last Mile</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">Between a well-designed intervention and the household it is meant to reach stands a person: a woman who walks to a hamlet with a register and a bag of supplies, who is neither a bureaucrat nor a volunteer but something engineered in between. The community health worker, the anganwadi worker, the <em>shasthya shebika</em> &mdash; these are not incidental helpers. They are <strong>designed human infrastructure</strong>, and the choices a designer makes about how to select, pay, supervise, and equip them decide whether the last mile is crossed or merely mapped.</p></div>
<h3>The Frontline Cadre as a Design Object</h3>
<p>When the mechanism of an intervention is <em>a person doing a task in a place the system cannot easily see</em>, the cadre itself becomes the design. South Asia has built three of the largest such cadres on earth, each a distinct answer to the same question: how do you put a trusted, trained agent within walking distance of every poor household?</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_User.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Three cadres, one design problem</div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">ASHA (India, NRHM 2005&ndash;)</div><div class="definition-text">The Accredited Social Health Activist &mdash; a local woman selected by her own village, paid mostly by task-based incentives, bridging households to the public health system. Roughly one million now serve across India.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Anganwadi worker (India, ICDS)</div><div class="definition-text">Runs the village child-care centre under the Integrated Child Development Services &mdash; nutrition, pre-school, growth monitoring &mdash; on a fixed monthly honorarium rather than piece rates.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Shasthya shebika (Bangladesh, BRAC)</div><div class="definition-text">BRAC&rsquo;s community health volunteer, who earns a margin by selling essential health commodities &mdash; a market incentive layered onto a social mission.</div></div></div>
<p>Note that all three are women, local, and part-time. That is not sentiment; it is design. Local selection buys trust and lowers attrition; part-time work keeps the wage bill payable at population scale; a woman can enter homes and speak to mothers where a male official cannot. Every one of these design choices also creates a constraint the rest of the intervention must live with.</p>
<h3>Paying the Frontline: Three Incentive Models</h3>
<p>How you pay a frontline agent silently rewrites what they do. Economists call this the multitasking problem (Holmstrom &amp; Milgrom): when a worker has many duties but you can only measure and reward some, effort flows to the measured tasks and drains from the rest. Each pay model buys something and distorts something.</p>
<div class="comparison-cards">
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Target.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></span><span class="comparison-title">Piece-rate / performance pay</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p><strong>Buys:</strong> effort on countable outputs &mdash; institutional deliveries, immunisations. <strong>Distorts:</strong> effort skews to paid tasks; unpaid duties (counselling, follow-up) are neglected; incentive to over-report or cream easy cases. ASHA&rsquo;s task-based schedule is the classic instance.</p></div></div>
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Clipboard.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></span><span class="comparison-title">Fixed salary / honorarium</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p><strong>Buys:</strong> predictability, willingness to do unmeasured relational work. <strong>Distorts:</strong> weak link between effort and reward invites absenteeism and shirking unless supervision is real. The anganwadi honorarium sits here.</p></div></div>
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Spark.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></span><span class="comparison-title">Mission / social motivation</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p><strong>Buys:</strong> intrinsic commitment, self-selection of the mission-minded. <strong>Distorts:</strong> unreliable and unscalable alone; crowded out if crude cash incentives are bolted on; burns out when the workload rises without recognition.</p></div></div>
</div>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Spark.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> There is no clean pay model &mdash; only a chosen distortion</div><p>Every incentive scheme rewards what it can count and quietly taxes what it cannot. The designer&rsquo;s job is not to find the undistorting scheme &mdash; there is none &mdash; but to make the <em>measured</em> tasks the ones that actually matter, and to protect the unmeasured relational work with supervision and mission, not cash alone.</p></div>
<h3>The Delivery Chain: A Cadre Is Only as Strong as Its Weakest Link</h3>
<p>J-PAL&rsquo;s delivery-chain lens treats the cadre not as a hiring decision but as a sequence of links, each of which must hold: <strong>selection &rarr; training &rarr; supervision &rarr; supply &rarr; retention</strong>. A brilliant incentive is worthless if the drug stock is empty; excellent training decays without supervision; motivated recruits leave if the promised honorarium arrives four months late.</p>
<div class="table-wrapper"><table><thead><tr><th>Link</th><th>Design question</th><th>Failure when it breaks</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Selection</td><td>Who is chosen, and by whom?</td><td>Elite capture of the post; workers distrusted by the very households they serve.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Training</td><td>Once, or continuous? Skills or scripts?</td><td>Knowledge decay; workers who can recite but cannot counsel.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Supervision</td><td>Who checks the work, how often, with what data?</td><td>Absenteeism and ghost activity; no correction loop.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Supply chain</td><td>Do the commodities arrive on time?</td><td>The worker shows up; the vaccine, the ORS, the register does not.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Retention</td><td>Is the role worth staying in?</td><td>Attrition erases the training investment and the household trust with it.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p>Because the links multiply rather than add, coverage collapses fast. If selection, training, supervision, supply, and retention each work 80% of the time, end-to-end delivery is 0.8&#8309; &asymp; 33%. Strengthening the strongest link changes nothing; only the weakest link moves the outcome. Diagnose the chain before you redesign the incentive.</p></div></div>
<h3>Absenteeism and the Limits of Monitoring</h3>
<p>The most-cited frontline failure is simply not being there. In the landmark six-country study &ldquo;Missing in Action&rdquo; (Chaudhury et al. 2006), on any given day <strong>roughly a quarter of teachers and health workers were absent</strong> from the facilities they were paid to staff &mdash; and absence was highest where supervision and consequences were weakest.</p>
<div class="stat-grid">
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">~1M</div><div class="stat-label">ASHAs deployed across India under the NRHM/NHM</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">~25%</div><div class="stat-label">Average absence of teachers &amp; health workers, six-country study (Chaudhury et al. 2006)</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">~40%</div><div class="stat-label">Health-worker absence in the Indian sample &mdash; among the highest observed</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">~1.4M</div><div class="stat-label">Anganwadi centres in the ICDS network</div></div>
</div>
<p>Monitoring can move the number &mdash; camera-and-pay experiments in Rajasthan cut teacher absence sharply (Banerjee et al. 2008) &mdash; but only where the measured presence is tied to real consequences and the task you monitor is the task that matters. Monitoring fixes <em>attendance</em>; it does not by itself buy <em>effort</em>, and a worker present but disengaged can satisfy the camera while failing the household.</p>
<div class="callout callout-blue"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Info.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p>Monitoring is a substitute for weak intrinsic motivation, not a complement to it. Layer heavy surveillance onto a mission-driven cadre and you can crowd out the very commitment that made them cheap to motivate. Match the accountability instrument to <em>why</em> the worker is failing &mdash; absence, ignorance, or empty shelves are three different problems.</p></div></div>
<h3>Digital Tools at the Frontline: Help or Just Burden?</h3>
<p>The reflex fix for a struggling cadre is now an app &mdash; a dashboard for the supervisor, a data-entry form for the worker. Sometimes it closes a real gap; often it adds a reporting burden that steals time from the household without improving the outcome.</p>
<div class="card-grid">
<div class="card"><div class="card-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Arrow_upward.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><p><strong>When it helps:</strong> it removes a real friction the worker feels &mdash; a job aid that guides counselling, an automatic reminder for the next home visit, a stock alert that keeps the supply link full.</p></div>
<div class="card"><div class="card-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><p><strong>When it burdens:</strong> it serves the dashboard, not the worker &mdash; duplicate registers, poor connectivity, data entered for someone else&rsquo;s report while the mother waits or is skipped.</p></div>
<div class="card"><div class="card-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Settings.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><p><strong>The test:</strong> does the tool reduce the worker&rsquo;s effort per useful task, or shift effort toward being measured? Design for the frontline user first, the analyst second.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="worked-example"><div class="worked-example-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Crosshair_detailed.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Case &mdash; designing ASHA incentives</div>
<p><strong>The problem.</strong> An ASHA earns a fixed incentive per institutional delivery she accompanies but little or nothing for the slow relational work &mdash; antenatal counselling, newborn home visits, tracking a child through the full immunisation schedule. Predictably, effort concentrates on the paid, countable task.</p>
<p><strong>The multitasking trap.</strong> Deliveries rise on the dashboard; newborn-care visits and immunisation follow-up lag, because they are unpaid and unmeasured. The scheme has bought the task it can count and taxed the tasks that quietly save lives.</p>
<p><strong>Redesign, chain-first.</strong> Before touching the incentive, check the links: are payments arriving on time (retention), is there a supervisor reviewing her register (supervision), is the immunisation stock in the sub-centre (supply)? Then rebalance incentives toward a small number of high-value <em>outcomes</em> &mdash; a completed immunisation series, a tracked newborn &mdash; not just contacts, while protecting counselling with supervision and peer recognition rather than a fee per sentence spoken.</p>
<p><strong>The design lesson.</strong> You cannot pay for everything, so decide deliberately what the measured tasks will be &mdash; and make them the ones that matter most. The incentive is a steering wheel, not an accelerator.</p></div>
<div class="reflection-prompt"><p><strong>Reflect.</strong> Take one frontline cadre you know. Which pay model does it use, and which unmeasured task is being quietly starved as a result? Now walk its delivery chain &mdash; selection, training, supervision, supply, retention &mdash; and name the weakest link. Would an app strengthen that link, or just add a report on top of it?</p></div>
<blockquote>&ldquo;The last mile is not a distance. It is a person &mdash; and whether the intervention arrives depends on how you designed the terms of her work.&rdquo;<cite>&mdash; after the J-PAL delivery-chain framework</cite></blockquote>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Test your grasp of frontline human infrastructure &mdash; incentives, delivery chains, and the last mile &mdash; before moving on.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> An ASHA is paid a per-delivery incentive but nothing for newborn home visits, and her home-visit numbers stay low. This is best described as: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m6q1" id="m6q1a" value="a"><label for="m6q1a">Elite capture of the frontline post.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m6q1" id="m6q1b" value="b"><label for="m6q1b">A supply-chain failure in the delivery system.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m6q1" id="m6q1c" value="c"><label for="m6q1c">The multitasking problem &mdash; effort flows to the paid, measured task and drains from the unpaid one.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m6q1" id="m6q1d" value="d"><label for="m6q1d">Proof that performance pay never works for health workers.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> In the delivery-chain lens (selection, training, supervision, supply, retention), why does strengthening the strongest link often change nothing? <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m6q2" id="m6q2a" value="a"><label for="m6q2a">Because the links add up, so any single link barely matters.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m6q2" id="m6q2b" value="b"><label for="m6q2b">Because the links multiply, so end-to-end delivery is capped by the weakest link.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m6q2" id="m6q2c" value="c"><label for="m6q2c">Because supervision is always the only link that matters.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m6q2" id="m6q2d" value="d"><label for="m6q2d">Because digital dashboards make all links equally strong.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> The &ldquo;Missing in Action&rdquo; study (Chaudhury et al. 2006) and the monitoring evidence together suggest that camera-and-pay monitoring: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m6q3" id="m6q3a" value="a"><label for="m6q3a">Can reduce absence when presence is tied to real consequences, but does not by itself guarantee effort.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m6q3" id="m6q3b" value="b"><label for="m6q3b">Eliminates both absence and disengagement completely.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m6q3" id="m6q3c" value="c"><label for="m6q3c">Always improves outcomes because more data is always better.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m6q3" id="m6q3d" value="d"><label for="m6q3d">Has no measurable effect on frontline attendance anywhere.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Take a frontline cadre you know. Which incentive model does it use, which unmeasured task is being starved, and would tighter monitoring fix the real reason it is failing &mdash; absence, weak skills, or an empty supply shelf?</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('intervention', 7, 'Module 7: Institutions of the Poor — SHGs, Federations & Collective Models',
  'Self-help groups and NRLM, producer collectives and FPOs, and the design of community institutions that reach population scale — with the failure modes of elite capture and free-riding.',
  $html$<h2>Module 7: Institutions of the Poor &mdash; SHGs, Federations &amp; Collective Models</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">Some of the most durable interventions in South Asia are not services delivered <em>to</em> the poor but institutions built <em>by</em> them. A self-help group of fifteen women who save a few rupees a week is, on paper, a tiny finance arrangement. Designed well and stacked into federations, it becomes the delivery rail for credit, livelihoods, entitlements and voice at the scale of tens of millions of households. This module treats the self-help group not as a microfinance product but as a designed institution &mdash; and asks what makes collective institutions of the poor endure rather than capture, corrode or collapse.</p></div>
<h3>The SHG as an Institution, Not a Loan</h3>
<p>The self-help group is a small, self-selected affinity group &mdash; usually 10&ndash;20 women &mdash; that meets regularly, pools savings, and lends internally before it ever touches a bank. The financial flows matter, but the design object is the <em>group as an institution</em>: a place where poor women accumulate a first asset (savings), practise rotating leadership, keep records, and learn to act collectively. Credit is what the institution does; it is not what the institution <em>is</em>.</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_User.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Anatomy of the design</div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Savings first</div><div class="definition-text">The group begins by saving, not borrowing. Members build a shared corpus and a credit history before external money arrives &mdash; skin in the game that disciplines lending.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Joint liability &amp; peer screening</div><div class="definition-text">Members know each other, so they screen and monitor at a cost no bank could bear. Social collateral substitutes for physical collateral.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">SHG&ndash;bank linkage</div><div class="definition-text">Pioneered by NABARD from 1992, the group is linked to a commercial or cooperative bank that lends to the group as a unit. The group, not the bank, does the last-mile allocation and recovery (NABARD 1992).</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Rules the group owns</div><div class="definition-text">Meeting norms, interest rates, sanctions for default &mdash; set and enforced by members, not imposed from outside. Ownership of the rules is what makes them stick.</div></div></div>
<p>This distinction is the whole design lesson of the module. A programme that treats the SHG as a channel for pushing loans will optimise disbursement and hollow out the institution. A programme that treats the SHG as an institution will invest in the slow work &mdash; savings, meetings, book-keeping, leadership rotation &mdash; and the credit follows as a symptom of institutional health.</p>
<h3>From Group to Federation: Building at Population Scale</h3>
<p>A single SHG is fragile: fifteen women cannot negotiate with a bank, run a grain bank, or hold a block official to account. The design answer is <em>nesting</em> &mdash; groups federate upward into larger bodies that do what no single group can. India's Deendayal Antyodaya Yojana &mdash; National Rural Livelihoods Mission (DAY-NRLM), launched 2011, made this the architecture of an anti-poverty programme at national scale.</p>
<div class="comparison-cards">
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon">1</span><span class="comparison-title">SHG</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p>10&ndash;20 women. Savings, internal lending, mutual support. The primary institution.</p></div></div>
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon">2</span><span class="comparison-title">Village Organisation</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p>A federation of 10&ndash;15 SHGs in a village. Manages larger funds, resolves disputes, links to schemes.</p></div></div>
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon">3</span><span class="comparison-title">Cluster / Block Federation</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p>Federates village organisations across a cluster or block. Negotiates with banks and the state; runs enterprises.</p></div></div>
</div>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Layers.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Federation is a design choice, not a growth accident</div><p>Federating is how a fragile local institution acquires bargaining power, professional staff and financial depth without losing member ownership. But each tier added is a tier that can be captured or can float free of its base. <strong>The design question at every level is the same: does this tier serve the groups below it, or does it start serving itself?</strong></p></div>
<h3>Ostrom's Design Principles, Mapped to SHG Federations</h3>
<p>Why do some collective institutions of the poor endure for decades while others dissolve the moment the facilitating agency leaves? Elinor Ostrom's study of long-lived commons institutions identified design principles that separate durable collective action from the tragedy of the commons (Ostrom 1990). They map almost directly onto SHG-federation design.</p>
<div class="table-wrapper"><table><thead><tr><th>Ostrom design principle</th><th>What it means</th><th>In SHG&ndash;federation design</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Clear boundaries</td><td>Who is a member, and who is not, is unambiguous.</td><td>Defined affinity group; poverty-targeted membership; a known roster.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rules fit local conditions</td><td>Rules match the resource and the people, not a template.</td><td>Interest rates and meeting norms set by the group, not the bank.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Collective-choice arrangements</td><td>Those affected by rules help make them.</td><td>Members vote on lending priorities; leadership rotates.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Monitoring</td><td>Monitors are accountable to members.</td><td>Book-keeper and office-bearers drawn from and answerable to the group.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Graduated sanctions</td><td>Penalties escalate with the offence; no zero-tolerance brittleness.</td><td>Late-payment fines before exclusion; peer pressure before default listing.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Conflict-resolution mechanisms</td><td>Cheap, fast, local dispute settlement.</td><td>Village organisation arbitrates disputes between groups and members.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nested enterprises</td><td>Small units nested in layers of larger ones.</td><td>SHG &rarr; village organisation &rarr; cluster federation.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h3>Producer Collectives, FPOs and the Cooperative Line</h3>
<p>The same logic &mdash; aggregate the poor so they can act at a scale the market respects &mdash; runs through producer institutions. A smallholder farmer selling alone is a price-taker at the mercy of the trader. A Farmer Producer Organisation (FPO) that aggregates the produce of thousands captures economies of scale in inputs, processing and marketing. The canonical case is dairy: Amul and the Anand pattern of village dairy cooperatives federated into district unions and a state marketing federation turned millions of one-buffalo households into the owners of a national brand.</p>
<div class="callout callout-green"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Arrow_upward.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Aggregation is the intervention.</strong> Amul did not make farmers better at keeping cattle; it changed their <em>position in the value chain</em> &mdash; from selling raw milk to a middleman to collectively owning the processing and the brand. FPOs promoted under NABARD and NRLM chase the same design: land titling and producer aggregation raise smallholder incomes precisely by shifting bargaining power, not by adding inputs (Deininger &amp; Liu 2013).</p></div></div>
<h3>Failure Modes: When Collective Institutions Corrode</h3>
<p>Collective institutions of the poor fail in patterned ways, and every failure is the flip side of a design principle ignored.</p>
<div class="card-grid">
<div class="card"><div class="card-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><p><strong>Elite capture.</strong> The better-off or dominant-caste members steer credit and posts to themselves. Boundaries and collective-choice rules exist on paper but not in practice.</p></div>
<div class="card"><div class="card-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><p><strong>Free-riding.</strong> Members enjoy the group's benefits without contributing to its upkeep. Absent monitoring and graduated sanctions, the diligent subsidise the idle until they quit.</p></div>
<div class="card"><div class="card-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><p><strong>Weak governance.</strong> Frozen leadership, unaudited books, ghost meetings &mdash; the institutional form without the function, an SHG that exists only for the loan.</p></div>
<div class="card"><div class="card-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><p><strong>Dependence on the promoter.</strong> The group runs only while the NGO or mission staffer is present and dissolves when they leave &mdash; community-driven development as a participatory fig leaf, not a self-sustaining institution.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="worked-example"><div class="worked-example-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Target.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Case &mdash; JEEViKA and Kudumbashree federation design</div>
<p>Bihar's JEEViKA (the state rural livelihoods mission, later folded into NRLM) and Kerala's Kudumbashree are the two most studied SHG-federation systems in India, and both illustrate the design principles above at work.</p>
<p><strong>Kudumbashree (Kerala, 1998).</strong> A three-tier structure &mdash; neighbourhood groups, area development societies, community development societies &mdash; deliberately fused with local government. The federation is not just a finance body; it holds a formal relationship with the panchayat, so the institution of the poor sits inside the institution of the state. Boundaries are sharp (poverty-targeted membership), leadership rotates, and disputes are resolved locally &mdash; Ostrom's nested-enterprise and conflict-resolution principles built into the architecture.</p>
<p><strong>JEEViKA (Bihar, 2007).</strong> Randomised evidence found that JEEViKA's SHGs raised savings and formal-sector borrowing and cut reliance on high-cost moneylenders &mdash; but the durable gains came where village organisations and cluster federations gave the groups real bargaining power, not where SHGs existed in name only. The Andhra Pradesh SERP model that preceded it showed the same: the federation, not the individual group, is what holds the institution up after the facilitating agency withdraws.</p>
<p>The design lesson: the SHG delivers when it is <em>built as a nested, member-owned institution</em> and captured or hollow when it is treated as a disbursement channel. Same tool, opposite outcomes &mdash; decided by design, before a rupee moved.</p></div>
<div class="stat-grid">
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">90M+</div><div class="stat-label">Rural women mobilised into SHGs under DAY-NRLM</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">8.8M</div><div class="stat-label">SHGs promoted across India under the mission</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">1992</div><div class="stat-label">NABARD launches the SHG&ndash;bank linkage programme</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">3 tiers</div><div class="stat-label">SHG &rarr; village organisation &rarr; cluster federation</div></div>
</div>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Flag.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>The honest test of community-driven development.</strong> Ask not "is there a group?" but "would this institution survive the promoter leaving tomorrow?" If the savings, the meetings, the book-keeping and the rule-enforcement are member-owned, it is an institution. If they run only on the facilitator's presence, it is scaffolding &mdash; and scaffolding is not a building.</p></div></div>
<div class="reflection-prompt"><p><strong>Reflect.</strong> Take one SHG or producer collective you know. Score it against Ostrom's seven design principles in the table &mdash; which are genuinely present, and which exist only on paper? Then ask the promoter-dependence question: would it survive if the NGO or mission staff withdrew this month? What single design change would most raise its odds of enduring?</p></div>
<blockquote>"There is no reason to believe that bureaucrats and politicians, no matter how well meaning, are better at solving problems than the people on the spot, who have the strongest incentive to get the solution right."<cite>&mdash; Elinor Ostrom</cite></blockquote>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Test your grasp of collective institutions of the poor before moving on.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> What is the central design insight in treating a self-help group as an <em>institution</em> rather than a loan channel? <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m7q1" id="m7q1a" value="a"><label for="m7q1a">Loans should be as large as possible to maximise impact.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m7q1" id="m7q1b" value="b"><label for="m7q1b">Banks, not members, should set the group's rules to ensure discipline.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m7q1" id="m7q1c" value="c"><label for="m7q1c">Investing in savings, meetings, book-keeping and member-owned rules builds a durable institution, and credit follows as a symptom of its health.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m7q1" id="m7q1d" value="d"><label for="m7q1d">The group only matters as a way to speed up loan disbursement.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> Which of Ostrom's design principles is most directly reflected in the SHG &rarr; village organisation &rarr; cluster federation structure? <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m7q2" id="m7q2a" value="a"><label for="m7q2a">Clear boundaries.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m7q2" id="m7q2b" value="b"><label for="m7q2b">Nested enterprises &mdash; small units organised in layers of larger ones.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m7q2" id="m7q2c" value="c"><label for="m7q2c">Graduated sanctions.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m7q2" id="m7q2d" value="d"><label for="m7q2d">Monitoring.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="d"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> A collective institution runs well while the promoting NGO is present but dissolves the moment its staff leave. This is best described as: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m7q3" id="m7q3a" value="a"><label for="m7q3a">Successful aggregation of producers.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m7q3" id="m7q3b" value="b"><label for="m7q3b">Graduated sanctions working as designed.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m7q3" id="m7q3c" value="c"><label for="m7q3c">Effective SHG&ndash;bank linkage.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m7q3" id="m7q3d" value="d"><label for="m7q3d">Dependence on the promoter &mdash; scaffolding, not a self-sustaining institution.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Take one SHG, federation or producer collective you know. Which single Ostrom design principle is weakest in it, and what would fixing that principle change about how the institution behaves under stress?</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('intervention', 8, 'Module 8: Getting the Level Right — Service-Delivery Redesign',
  'Teaching at the Right Level, the cheap-robust-scalable archetypes (deworming, chlorination), and the genuine reach — and hard limits — of behavioural nudges and choice architecture.',
  $html$<h2>Module 8: Getting the Level Right &mdash; Service-Delivery Redesign</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">Some of the most powerful interventions in development add nothing new. They spend no more money, hire no new cadre, build no new building. Instead they <strong>redesign the mechanism of an existing service</strong> &mdash; how a classroom is grouped, how safe water reaches a jerrycan, how a default is set &mdash; so that the same inputs produce a far larger change in outcome. This module is about that family: service-delivery redesign, where the leverage lies not in more, but in a better-shaped delivery platform.</p></div>
<h3>Redesign the Mechanism, Not the Inputs</h3>
<p>Module 1 drew the line between adding inputs and redesigning the mechanism. This module lives on the second side of that line. The canonical case is <strong>Teaching at the Right Level (TaRL)</strong>, developed by Pratham and evaluated with J-PAL across India. The problem it attacks is the "schooling is not learning" gap: children sit in class for years, sorted by age and grade, while instruction sails over the heads of those who cannot yet read a paragraph or subtract two-digit numbers.</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Target.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> What TaRL actually changes</div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">The old mechanism</div><div class="definition-text">Group children by <em>age and grade</em>; teach the prescribed curriculum for that grade to all of them at once, regardless of what each can do.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">The redesigned mechanism</div><div class="definition-text">Assess each child, regroup by <em>current learning level</em>, and run a focused daily block of activities pitched exactly at that level &mdash; then move children up as they master each stage.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">What did <em>not</em> change</div><div class="definition-text">The same teachers, roughly the same hours, the same building and budget. No new inputs were added; the delivery platform was re-shaped.</div></div></div>
<p>Across randomised evaluations the reading and arithmetic gains were large and replicated in multiple forms &mdash; from Pratham volunteers to in-school "learning camps" to government-teacher delivery (Banerjee et al. 2007, 2017). The intervention worked because it removed a <em>binding constraint</em> &mdash; mismatch between instruction and level &mdash; that no volume of extra textbooks could touch.</p>
<div class="comparison-cards">
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Layers.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></span><span class="comparison-title">Add inputs</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p>More teachers, textbooks, classrooms, tablets. Cost rises linearly. Works only if the missing input was the binding constraint &mdash; often it is not.</p></div></div>
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Crosshair_detailed.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></span><span class="comparison-title">Redesign mechanism</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p>Same inputs, re-routed. Regroup by level, reorder the queue, reset the default. Cost is near-flat; the gain comes from removing the true constraint.</p></div></div>
</div>
<h3>The "Cheap, Robust, Scalable" Archetype</h3>
<p>A second family of redesigns wins not by cleverness of targeting but by being <em>almost free per unit of outcome</em>, working across many contexts, and being trivial to deliver. Two examples anchor the literature.</p>
<div class="callout callout-green"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Spark.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Mass deworming.</strong> Treating whole schools for intestinal worms &mdash; rather than screening and treating individuals &mdash; cut absenteeism and raised school participation at a cost of pennies per child, with spillover benefits to untreated children nearby (Miguel &amp; Kremer 2004). The redesign was the <em>delivery rule</em>: treat everyone through the school platform, skip the expensive diagnosis.</p></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-blue"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Info.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Chlorine dispensers.</strong> Placing a free chlorine dispenser at the water source &mdash; where people already collect water &mdash; sharply raised safe-water use versus selling bottled chlorine, by removing price, travel, and memory frictions all at once (Kremer et al.). Again, the intervention was a redesign of <em>where and how</em> the service met people, not a new product.</p></div></div>
<div class="stat-grid">
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">~$0.50</div><div class="stat-label">Order-of-magnitude cost to deworm one child for a year &mdash; among the most cost-effective health interventions measured</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">2&ndash;3x</div><div class="stat-label">Learning gain per dollar from TaRL-style remedial instruction versus adding conventional inputs</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">Whole-school</div><div class="stat-label">Deworming and dispenser models treat the platform, not the individual &mdash; cutting per-unit delivery cost</div></div>
</div>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Arrow_upward.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Cost-effectiveness is a design choice, not an accident</div><p>What deworming, dispensers, and TaRL share is that they push <em>cost per unit of outcome</em> toward zero by re-shaping delivery rather than buying more. This is why they scale: a mechanism that is cheap, robust to context, and simple to administer survives the journey from pilot to system that kills more elaborate designs (Module 11).</p></div>
<h3>Nudges and Choice Architecture &mdash; Real Reach, Real Limits</h3>
<p>The lightest redesigns change nothing about the service except the <em>choice environment</em> around it: the default option, a timely reminder, a small friction removed or added. Set immunisation or savings as the default; send an SMS reminder before a clinic visit; place the healthy or safe option first. These cost almost nothing and can move behaviour at genuine population scale.</p>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Know the ceiling.</strong> Nudges shift behaviour <em>at the margin</em> &mdash; effect sizes are usually modest, and they cannot fix a structural or supply-side failure. A reminder to visit a clinic is worthless if the clinic has no nurse or no drugs; a savings default cannot conjure income that is not there. Choice architecture works when the barrier is attention, friction, or inertia &mdash; and fails when the barrier is a missing service. Diagnose the constraint (Module 2) before reaching for a nudge.</p></div></div>
<h3>Platforms vs Point Solutions</h3>
<p>The deepest lesson of this module is the difference between <em>bolting a fix onto a service</em> and <em>redesigning the platform through which the service is delivered</em>. A point solution &mdash; one more app, one more training, one more kit &mdash; leaves the delivery mechanism untouched and usually disappoints. A platform redesign changes the mechanism itself: the classroom becomes level-grouped, the clinic queue is reordered, the water point becomes the treatment point.</p>
<div class="table-wrapper"><table><thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Point solution (add-on)</th><th>Platform redesign</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>What it touches</td><td>A single input bolted onto the existing service</td><td>The delivery mechanism the whole service runs on</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cost curve</td><td>Rises with each unit added</td><td>Near-flat &mdash; same inputs re-routed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical example</td><td>Distribute more textbooks; add a health app</td><td>TaRL level-grouping; whole-school deworming; source-based dispensers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Scaling behaviour</td><td>Often loses voltage; treats a symptom</td><td>Robust when the redesigned mechanism removed the binding constraint</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="worked-example"><div class="worked-example-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Clipboard.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Case &mdash; TaRL scaling in an Indian state (Haryana / Mission Buniyaad)</div>
<p>The proof-of-concept was clear from Pratham's evaluations; the open question was whether the redesigned mechanism could survive inside a government school system, delivered by regular teachers rather than trusted volunteers.</p>
<p><strong>The redesign at scale.</strong> In state partnerships &mdash; Haryana, and later the Delhi-region Mission Buniyaad programme &mdash; government teachers were trained to assess children, regroup them by learning level, and run a dedicated daily remedial block, with Pratham providing mentoring and a simple assessment rhythm. The platform, not the workforce, was re-engineered.</p>
<p><strong>What made or broke it.</strong> Where the daily level-grouped block genuinely happened, learning rose. Where it was diluted back into ordinary grade-based teaching &mdash; because the timetable, the official curriculum pressure, or supervision did not protect the block &mdash; the gains faded. The mechanism was cheap and robust <em>only when the delivery platform actually adopted it</em>; a nominal rollout that kept the old grouping was isomorphic mimicry (Module 1).</p>
<p>The lesson for scaling: a mechanism redesign is not self-executing. It scales only if the receiving system re-shapes its routine &mdash; the timetable, the grouping, the assessment &mdash; around the new mechanism, not just around its name.</p></div>
<div class="reflection-prompt"><p><strong>Reflect.</strong> Take a service you know &mdash; a school, a clinic, a ration shop. Is the improvement you would propose a <em>point solution</em> that adds an input, or a <em>platform redesign</em> that re-routes the same inputs? What is the binding constraint, and would a nudge, a delivery-rule change, or a full regrouping actually remove it?</p></div>
<blockquote>"The question is rarely whether to do more, but whether to do the same thing in a different shape &mdash; and whether the system will keep that shape once you leave."<cite>&mdash; after Banerjee, Duflo &amp; the TaRL evidence</cite></blockquote>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Test your grasp of service-delivery redesign before moving on to delivery systems and the last mile.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> What makes Teaching at the Right Level (TaRL) an example of <em>redesigning the mechanism</em> rather than adding inputs? <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m8q1" id="m8q1a" value="a"><label for="m8q1a">It hires additional trained teachers and buys new textbooks for every grade.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m8q1" id="m8q1b" value="b"><label for="m8q1b">It regroups children by current learning level for a focused daily block, using the same teachers, hours, and budget.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m8q1" id="m8q1c" value="c"><label for="m8q1c">It replaces classroom teaching entirely with tablets and software.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m8q1" id="m8q1d" value="d"><label for="m8q1d">It increases the number of school hours in the day.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> Mass deworming (Miguel &amp; Kremer 2004) and source-based chlorine dispensers are grouped together as a "cheap, robust, scalable" archetype because both: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m8q2" id="m8q2a" value="a"><label for="m8q2a">Rely on individually screening and diagnosing each beneficiary before treatment.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m8q2" id="m8q2b" value="b"><label for="m8q2b">Depend on a highly trained specialist cadre to deliver.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m8q2" id="m8q2c" value="c"><label for="m8q2c">Redesign the delivery rule to reach people through an existing platform at very low cost per unit of outcome.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m8q2" id="m8q2d" value="d"><label for="m8q2d">Work only in a single, carefully controlled context.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> A designer proposes an SMS reminder nudge to raise clinic attendance in an area where the clinic frequently has no nurse and no drugs. The most likely outcome is: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m8q3" id="m8q3a" value="a"><label for="m8q3a">Little effect, because a nudge shifts behaviour at the margin and cannot fix a supply-side failure.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m8q3" id="m8q3b" value="b"><label for="m8q3b">A large effect, because nudges reliably solve structural service gaps.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m8q3" id="m8q3c" value="c"><label for="m8q3c">The clinic will automatically be staffed once demand rises.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m8q3" id="m8q3d" value="d"><label for="m8q3d">Nudges are always ineffective and should never be used.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Think of a public service near you. Would the biggest gain come from adding an input, changing a default or delivery rule, or fully regrouping the delivery platform &mdash; and what binding constraint does each option actually remove?</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('intervention', 9, 'Module 9: The Delivery Chain',
  'Mapping every link from design intent to beneficiary outcome, finding where interventions leak, and treating state capacity as a binding design constraint rather than an afterthought.',
  $html$<h2>Module 9: The Delivery Chain</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">A well-designed intervention can still fail to reach a single beneficiary, because between the designer&rsquo;s intent and the change in someone&rsquo;s life lies a chain of hand-offs &mdash; funds, staff, inputs, the service act itself, uptake. Each link is imperfect, and the imperfections <em>multiply</em>. This module treats the delivery chain as a first-class design object: something you map, stress-test, and design <em>for</em>, rather than an implementation detail to be sorted out later by someone else. The uncomfortable lesson of &ldquo;deliverology&rdquo; (Barber 2015) is that most of the distance between a good idea and a good outcome is lost here.</p></div>
<h3>Mapping the Chain: Intent to Outcome</h3>
<p>Every intervention runs a relay from the designer&rsquo;s intent to the beneficiary&rsquo;s outcome. Name each hand-off, because a break at any one of them stops the whole race &mdash; and the links are rarely the ones a logframe emphasises.</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Layers.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> The seven links from design to outcome</div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Intent</div><div class="definition-text">The design as written &mdash; the mechanism and target the theory of change specifies (Module 3).</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Funds</div><div class="definition-text">Money released, on time, to the right account. In India&rsquo;s schemes this link alone routinely stalls for months.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Staff</div><div class="definition-text">A frontline worker present, trained, and motivated to act &mdash; the teacher, ANM, ration dealer, or gram rozgar sahayak.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Inputs</div><div class="definition-text">The physical thing in the right place &mdash; grain in the fair-price shop, a vaccine at the right temperature, a textbook.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Service act</div><div class="definition-text">The intended transaction actually performed &mdash; the grain weighed out honestly, the class taught, the wage paid.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Uptake</div><div class="definition-text">The beneficiary reached, eligible, and willing to use the service &mdash; not deterred by distance, stigma, or a failed authentication.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Outcome</div><div class="definition-text">The change the intervention exists to produce &mdash; nutrition, learning, income &mdash; which only appears if all prior links hold.</div></div></div>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Spark.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> The chain is multiplicative, not additive</div><p>Delivery is a product of probabilities, not a sum. If five links each work 80&percnt; of the time &mdash; individually respectable &mdash; the end-to-end pass rate is 0.8&sup5; &asymp; <strong>33&percnt;</strong>. Two-thirds of the intended effect vanishes without any single link &ldquo;failing.&rdquo; This is why marginal effort is often best spent on the <em>weakest</em> link, not the most visible one, and why designers must know their real per-link odds.</p></div>
<div class="stat-grid">
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">0.8&sup5;</div><div class="stat-label">Five links at 80&percnt; each leave roughly 33&percnt; of intended effect</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">~50&percnt;</div><div class="stat-label">Share of subsidised PDS grain that failed to reach households in the mid-2000s (Khera 2011)</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">25&percnt;</div><div class="stat-label">Primary-school teachers found absent on any given day (Chaudhury et al. 2006)</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">1 in 4</div><div class="stat-label">Health workers absent in the same multi-country study &mdash; a broken staff link</div></div>
</div>
<h3>Where Interventions Leak: The Last Mile and the Missing Middle</h3>
<p>Leakage clusters in two places. The <em>last mile</em> &mdash; the final hand-off to the beneficiary &mdash; gets most of the attention: the ration dealer who under-weighs, the ghost beneficiary drawing another&rsquo;s entitlement. But as much damage is done in the <em>missing middle</em>: the district and block administration where funds sit undisbursed, muster rolls are inflated, and no one owns the outcome.</p>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Ghost beneficiaries and diverted grain.</strong> In the classic PDS, subsidised grain leaked through duplicate and fictitious ration cards and diversion into the open market &mdash; a last-mile and record-keeping failure at once. In MGNREGA, fake names on muster rolls converted a wage-employment guarantee into a rent for local officials. The design was sound; the chain was porous.</p></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-red"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Delayed funds are a design failure, not an accident.</strong> MGNREGA guarantees wages within fifteen days, yet chronic delays in fund flow from centre to state to panchayat broke the incentive at the core of the scheme. A worker who cannot count on timely payment stops demanding work &mdash; so the missing middle silently reduces <em>uptake</em>, the very last link, without anyone at the last mile doing wrong.</p></div></div>
<h3>State Capacity as a Binding Design Constraint</h3>
<p>The deepest lesson of the delivery chain is that you must design for the delivery system you <em>actually have</em>, not the idealised one your theory of change imagines. A design that assumes capacity which is absent will fail regardless of how sound its internal logic is. State capability &mdash; the administrative ability to implement &mdash; is a binding constraint, not a variable you can wish away (Andrews, Pritchett &amp; Woolcock 2017).</p>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Isomorphic mimicry, revisited</div><div class="definition-text">Weak systems adopt the <em>forms</em> of capable ones &mdash; elaborate MIS dashboards, social audits on paper &mdash; while the underlying function stays broken. A design that looks modern can quietly demand capability the chain does not have.</div></div>
<div class="callout callout-purple"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Layers.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Design test.</strong> For every link in your chain, ask: does a real person, in a real office, with real workload and skills, have both the <em>ability</em> and the <em>incentive</em> to perform this act? If the answer rests on a district officer doing five extra hours of unpaid reconciliation each week, the link is fictional. Simplify the design until the chain fits the system, or invest first in the capability the design needs.</p></div></div>
<h3>Fidelity versus Adaptation: The Central Tension</h3>
<p>Once a design meets a real delivery system, every intervention faces the same knife-edge. Implement it too rigidly and it does not fit local reality; implement it too loosely and the mechanism that made it work dissolves. Managing this tension &mdash; deciding which elements are the non-negotiable &ldquo;active ingredient&rdquo; and which are free to flex &mdash; is a core delivery-chain design decision.</p>
<div class="comparison-cards">
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Settings.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></span><span class="comparison-title">High fidelity</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p>Preserves the active mechanism exactly as tested.</p><p>Protects against the model being hollowed out en route to scale.</p><p>Risk: rigidity that ignores local constraints &mdash; committees that never meet, a protocol staff cannot follow.</p></div></div>
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_User.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></span><span class="comparison-title">Local adaptation</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p>Fits the intervention to language, geography, and the delivery system on the ground.</p><p>Builds frontline ownership and durability.</p><p>Risk: drift &mdash; adapt the active ingredient away and the mechanism no longer fires.</p></div></div>
</div>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Crosshair_detailed.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Be rigid about the mechanism, flexible about the form</div><p>Name the one or two elements that <em>are</em> the intervention &mdash; the thing your theory of change says does the causal work &mdash; and defend those with high fidelity. Let everything else adapt. A design that specifies both what must not change and what may change gives the delivery chain room to fit reality without dissolving the mechanism.</p></div>
<h3>Technology to Plug the Leaks &mdash; Gains and Side Effects</h3>
<p>The last decade&rsquo;s answer to a leaky chain has been to instrument it: biometric authentication and end-to-end computerisation to close the gaps where ghosts and diversion hide. It works &mdash; and it introduces a new failure at the uptake link that designers must weigh.</p>
<div class="worked-example"><div class="worked-example-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Clipboard.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Case &mdash; plugging PDS and MGNREGA leakage</div>
<p>The problem: subsidised grain and guaranteed wages leaked through ghost beneficiaries, over-invoicing, and diverted stock &mdash; a chain porous at the staff, service-act, and uptake links.</p>
<p><strong>The intervention.</strong> Aadhaar-enabled biometric authentication at the point of service, plus end-to-end computerisation of records and fund flow: the beneficiary&rsquo;s fingerprint is matched before grain is released or a wage is paid, and the transaction is logged in real time. In Andhra Pradesh, Muralidharan, Niehaus and Sukhtankar (2016) evaluated Smartcard-based biometric payments for MGNREGA and pensions in a large randomised rollout.</p>
<p><strong>The gain.</strong> Payments arrived faster and more reliably, and the leakage between what the government disbursed and what beneficiaries received fell sharply &mdash; the technology closed the last-mile and missing-middle gaps that audits alone had not. Beneficiaries valued the system enough that few wished to return to the old one.</p>
<p><strong>The side effect.</strong> Authentication that fails &mdash; a worn fingerprint, no network, a name mismatch &mdash; converts a leakage-plugging tool into an <em>exclusion error</em>: a genuine beneficiary denied their entitlement at the counter. Plugging a leak at the service-act link can quietly break the uptake link for the most vulnerable, who are least able to travel back or contest a rejection.</p></div>
<div class="callout callout-green"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Arrow_upward.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>The design trade-off is exclusion versus inclusion error.</strong> Tighter authentication reduces leakage (fewer ineligibles paid) but raises exclusion (more eligibles wrongly denied). A humane design plugs the leak <em>and</em> builds a fallback &mdash; manual override, offline mode, grievance redress &mdash; so the uptake link survives the technology meant to protect it.</p></div></div>
<div class="reflection-prompt"><p><strong>Reflect.</strong> Take one intervention you know and draw its delivery chain from intent to outcome. Estimate the real success probability of each link and multiply them &mdash; what fraction of the intended effect survives? Now name the single weakest link, and ask whether a technology fix there would plug a leak or open a new exclusion.</p></div>
<blockquote>&ldquo;The space between a policy decision and its effect on a citizen&rsquo;s life is where reform is won or lost.&rdquo;<cite>&mdash; after Michael Barber, <em>How to Run a Government</em> (2015)</cite></blockquote>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Test your grasp of the delivery chain before moving on to targeting and scale.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> A delivery chain has five sequential links, each of which works 80&percnt; of the time. What share of the intended effect reaches the beneficiary, and why? <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m9q1" id="m9q1a" value="a"><label for="m9q1a">80&percnt;, because the worst link sets the ceiling.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m9q1" id="m9q1b" value="b"><label for="m9q1b">About 60&percnt;, because the failures add up across links.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m9q1" id="m9q1c" value="c"><label for="m9q1c">About 33&percnt;, because the links are multiplicative (0.8&sup5;), not additive.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m9q1" id="m9q1d" value="d"><label for="m9q1d">100&percnt;, because no single link failed outright.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> An intervention&rsquo;s theory of change is sound, but it assumes district officials will perform hours of reconciliation the system has no capacity or incentive for. The best design response is: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m9q2" id="m9q2a" value="a"><label for="m9q2a">Keep the design and issue a stronger circular ordering officials to comply.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m9q2" id="m9q2b" value="b"><label for="m9q2b">Treat state capacity as a binding constraint and simplify the design to fit the system you actually have, or invest in the capability first.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m9q2" id="m9q2c" value="c"><label for="m9q2c">Scale it nationally quickly, before the capacity gap is noticed.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m9q2" id="m9q2d" value="d"><label for="m9q2d">Add an elaborate MIS dashboard so the reform at least looks modern.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="d"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> Aadhaar-enabled biometric authentication in PDS and MGNREGA (Muralidharan et al. 2016) reduced leakage. What is its characteristic side effect that a designer must plan for? <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m9q3" id="m9q3a" value="a"><label for="m9q3a">It increased leakage by making diversion easier.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m9q3" id="m9q3b" value="b"><label for="m9q3b">It slowed payments and made beneficiaries prefer the old system.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m9q3" id="m9q3c" value="c"><label for="m9q3c">It removed the need for any frontline staff at all.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m9q3" id="m9q3d" value="d"><label for="m9q3d">Authentication failures can wrongly deny genuine beneficiaries &mdash; an exclusion error at the uptake link.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Map the delivery chain of one intervention you have worked on. At which link did it leak most &mdash; the last mile or the missing middle &mdash; and would a technology fix there have plugged the leak or created a new exclusion?</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('intervention', 10, 'Module 10: Incentives, Targeting & Cost-Effectiveness',
  'Incentive compatibility across the delivery chain, inclusion versus exclusion errors in targeting, and cost-effectiveness as the discipline for choosing between competing interventions.',
  $html$<h2>Module 10: Incentives, Targeting &amp; Cost-Effectiveness</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">Three questions decide whether a well-theorised intervention survives contact with the real world. Will every actor in the delivery chain actually <em>want</em> to do their part? Are you reaching the people you meant to reach, and at what cost of trying? And is this the best use of the rupee, judged against the outcome you care about? This module treats incentives, targeting, and cost-effectiveness not as afterthoughts for the evaluation team but as design decisions &mdash; each one capable of quietly dismantling a mechanism that looked sound on paper.</p></div>
<h3>Incentive Compatibility Across the Delivery Chain</h3>
<p>An intervention is a chain of actors &mdash; the frontline worker, the supervising official, the vendor, the beneficiary &mdash; and each will act on their own incentives, not on your logframe. A design that asks any link to work <em>against</em> its own interest is a design that leaks. The discipline is to walk the chain and ask, at each node, <em>will this person find it in their interest to do their part?</em> Where the answer is no, no amount of training or exhortation will hold.</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_User.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Two classic traps</div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Principal&ndash;agent problem</div><div class="definition-text">The designer (principal) cannot observe everything the frontline agent does. The agent optimises for what is measured and rewarded &mdash; so an ASHA paid per institutional delivery pushes deliveries, and an official rewarded for disbursement speed disburses without checking eligibility.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Multitasking problem</div><div class="definition-text">When you reward one task, effort drains from the unrewarded ones. Pay teachers for test scores and they teach to the test; pay for enrolment and quality slides. Strong incentives on a narrow metric distort the wider job (Holmstrom &amp; Milgrom).</div></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Design that fights incentives loses.</strong> Before adding a monitoring rule or a penalty, ask whether the underlying incentive can be aligned instead. A vendor paid on delivery of a working borewell, not on drilling a hole, has no reason to walk away; a beneficiary whose cash transfer is unconditional has no reason to game a condition that was never worth enforcing.</p></div></div>
<h3>Targeting as a Design Trade-Off</h3>
<p>Targeting is where equity ambition meets administrative reality. Every targeting rule makes two kinds of mistake at once, and tightening against one loosens the other. There is no rule that eliminates both, so the design question is not "how do we target perfectly?" but "which error is cheaper to tolerate here, and does the cost of targeting exceed what it saves?"</p>
<div class="table-wrapper"><table><thead><tr><th>Error type</th><th>What happens</th><th>Who pays</th><th>When it is the lesser evil</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Inclusion error (leakage)</td><td>Non-poor households receive the benefit</td><td>The budget &mdash; money spent on people who did not need it</td><td>When the benefit is cheap per head and exclusion is morally or politically costly (e.g. subsidised staple grain)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exclusion error (undercoverage)</td><td>Genuinely poor households are screened out</td><td>The intended beneficiary &mdash; the person the intervention exists for</td><td>When the benefit is expensive per head and leakage would blow the budget</td></tr>
<tr><td>Targeting cost</td><td>Surveys, means tests, verification, appeals</td><td>The programme &mdash; and applicants who bear the hassle</td><td>Rarely worth it when the saving from tighter targeting is smaller than the cost of achieving it</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Spark.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> The cost of targeting can exceed the savings</div><p>India's debate over a universal Public Distribution System versus a tightly targeted one turns on exactly this. Fine means-testing sounds efficient, but proxy-means tests misclassify large shares of the poor, verification is expensive, and the excluded are precisely those least able to appeal. A broader, near-universal benefit accepts leakage in exchange for near-zero exclusion and low administrative cost &mdash; often the better design for a cheap, essential good. <strong>Targeting is a trade-off to be tuned to the benefit, not a virtue to be maximised.</strong> Module 7 treated the political economy of universal versus targeted; here it is a costed design choice.</p></div>
<h3>Cost-Effectiveness as a Design Discipline</h3>
<p>The most common error in comparing interventions is to compare cost per <em>output</em> &mdash; cost per training delivered, per kit distributed &mdash; when what you care about is cost per <em>outcome</em>. An intervention can be cheap per output and ruinously expensive per unit of the change you actually want. Cost-effectiveness analysis forces the denominator to be the outcome: cost per additional year of schooling, per DALY averted, per LAYS (learning-adjusted year of schooling).</p>
<div class="stat-grid">
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">$3</div><div class="stat-label">approx. cost per additional year of schooling &mdash; deworming where worm loads are high (Kremer et al. 2020)</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">$100+</div><div class="stat-label">cost per additional year of schooling &mdash; typical conditional cash transfer for attendance</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">~3&times;</div><div class="stat-label">difference in LAYS per dollar between the most and least cost-effective education programmes on the same outcome</div></div>
</div>
<div class="callout callout-blue"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Info.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Unit-cost thinking</strong> is a design habit, not just an evaluation output. Build the cost per outcome into the theory of change from the start: estimate the effect size the mechanism can plausibly deliver, divide the all-in cost by it, and see whether the number is defensible <em>before</em> you pilot. The sibling <em>Cost Effectiveness 101</em> flagship walks through building these ratios; the effect sizes they rely on come from the causal designs in the <em>MEL</em> and <em>Causal Inference</em> flagships.</p></div></div>
<div class="worked-example"><div class="worked-example-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Money.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Case &mdash; comparing two interventions on cost-effectiveness</div>
<p>A state education department has a fixed budget and one goal: more learning. Two proposals sit on the table, each rigorously evaluated, each reporting a real, positive effect.</p>
<p><strong>Proposal A &mdash; merit scholarships.</strong> Cash awards to high-scoring girls. All-in cost &#8377;4,000 per girl reached; the evaluation finds an average gain of 0.05 standard deviations in test scores across those reached. Cost per LAYS is high, because most of the spend flows to girls who would have progressed anyway &mdash; and the effect is thin.</p>
<p><strong>Proposal B &mdash; targeted remedial instruction.</strong> A Teaching-at-the-Right-Level block for the lowest-performing children. All-in cost &#8377;250 per child; the evaluation finds a gain of 0.15 standard deviations, concentrated on children furthest behind (Banerjee et al. 2017). Cost per LAYS is a fraction of Proposal A&rsquo;s.</p>
<p>Judged on cost per output &mdash; children reached &mdash; the two look comparable. Judged on cost per <em>outcome</em>, Proposal B delivers roughly an order of magnitude more learning per rupee, and delivers it to the children with the most to gain. The comparison only becomes visible once the denominator is the outcome, not the activity.</p></div>
<h3>Comparative Cost-Effectiveness &mdash; and Its Limits</h3>
<p>League tables of cost-effectiveness &mdash; GiveWell&rsquo;s charity rankings, 3ie&rsquo;s evidence syntheses &mdash; are powerful for triage. They can reveal that a neglected intervention buys ten times the outcome per dollar of a fashionable one. But a ratio is a guide, not a verdict, and treating it as one is its own design failure.</p>
<div class="comparison-cards">
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Arrow_upward.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></span><span class="comparison-title">What CEA does well</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p>Puts rival interventions on one axis; exposes cheap outputs masquerading as cheap outcomes; forces effect sizes and costs into the open; makes triage under a fixed budget honest.</p></div></div>
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></span><span class="comparison-title">Where it misleads</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p>Ratios travel badly &mdash; deworming&rsquo;s cost-effectiveness collapses where worm prevalence is low (external validity). It is blind to equity, to who gains, and to outcomes that resist a common metric. Contested effect sizes give falsely precise ratios.</p></div></div>
</div>
<div class="callout callout-purple"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Layers.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p>The deworming cost-effectiveness debate is the cautionary tale. The headline ratio was extraordinary, but it rested on one long-run effect estimate and on a prevalence context that does not generalise (Baird et al. 2016; the wider replication dispute). The lesson is not that CEA is useless &mdash; it is that <strong>a cost-effectiveness ratio is an input to judgement, weighted by external validity and equity, never a substitute for it.</strong></p></div></div>
<div class="reflection-prompt"><p><strong>Reflect.</strong> Take an intervention you are designing. Walk its delivery chain and name the one actor most likely to find their part <em>against</em> their interest &mdash; how would you realign it? Then state your targeting rule and say which error, inclusion or exclusion, you are choosing to tolerate and why. Finally, write your cost per outcome as a single number: is it defensible next to an alternative on the same outcome?</p></div>
<blockquote>&ldquo;The question is never simply &lsquo;does it work?&rsquo; but &lsquo;does it work, for whom, at what cost, compared to what else we could have done with the money?&rsquo;&rdquo;<cite>&mdash; after the cost-effectiveness tradition in development economics</cite></blockquote>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Test your grasp of incentives, targeting, and cost-effectiveness before moving on to scaling.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> A programme pays teachers a bonus tied only to their class&rsquo;s test scores, and finds that non-tested subjects and slower learners are neglected. This is best described as: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m10q1" id="m10q1a" value="a"><label for="m10q1a">An inclusion error in targeting.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m10q1" id="m10q1b" value="b"><label for="m10q1b">A cost-per-output miscalculation.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m10q1" id="m10q1c" value="c"><label for="m10q1c">A multitasking problem &mdash; rewarding one narrow metric drains effort from the unrewarded tasks.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m10q1" id="m10q1d" value="d"><label for="m10q1d">Isomorphic mimicry of a successful model.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> A benefit is cheap per head and essential, but fine means-testing misclassifies many poor households and is costly to administer. The strongest design argument is to: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m10q2" id="m10q2a" value="a"><label for="m10q2a">Tighten targeting further, since any leakage is wasted money.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m10q2" id="m10q2b" value="b"><label for="m10q2b">Accept some inclusion error in exchange for near-zero exclusion and low administrative cost.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m10q2" id="m10q2c" value="c"><label for="m10q2c">Cancel the programme because both errors cannot be eliminated.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m10q2" id="m10q2d" value="d"><label for="m10q2d">Add more verification steps to reduce the hassle cost.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> Two education programmes cost the same per child reached, but one reports triple the learning-adjusted years of schooling per dollar. The most accurate reading is that: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m10q3" id="m10q3a" value="a"><label for="m10q3a">Cost per output looked equal, but cost per outcome &mdash; the metric that matters &mdash; strongly favours one, though external validity and equity still need checking.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m10q3" id="m10q3b" value="b"><label for="m10q3b">The two programmes are equivalent because their cost per child is identical.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m10q3" id="m10q3c" value="c"><label for="m10q3c">The higher-ratio programme should be scaled everywhere without further judgement.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m10q3" id="m10q3d" value="d"><label for="m10q3d">Cost-effectiveness ratios are meaningless and should be ignored.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Take an intervention you know. Name the one delivery-chain actor whose incentives are least aligned, state which targeting error you would choose to tolerate, and write its cost per outcome as a single number you could defend against an alternative.</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('intervention', 11, 'Module 11: Why Things Don''t Scale — The Voltage Effect',
  'List''s five voltage drops, the mechanics of pilot-to-scale collapse, and the difference between evidence that will survive scaling and evidence that will not.',
  $html$<h2>Module 11: Why Things Don&rsquo;t Scale &mdash; The Voltage Effect</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">Here is the most disorienting fact in development practice: an intervention can produce large, real, well-measured effects in a trial and then produce almost nothing when a government runs it at national scale. The effect did not shrink because the idea was wrong. It shrank because the conditions that generated the effect &mdash; the people, the implementers, the small size itself &mdash; do not survive multiplication. John List calls this loss of effect the <strong>voltage drop</strong>, and learning to predict it before you scale is the intellectual core of intervention design (List 2022).</p></div>
<h3>The Voltage Metaphor, Stated Precisely</h3>
<p>Electrical voltage falls as current travels along a wire; the further it goes, the weaker it arrives. List borrows the image for the gap between the effect an intervention shows in a pilot and the effect that reaches people once it is scaled. The question a designer must ask is not merely &ldquo;does it work?&rdquo; but &ldquo;<em>will it keep its voltage?</em>&rdquo; Al-Ubaydli, List and Suskind call this <em>the science of using science</em> &mdash; the discipline of judging, in advance, whether a piece of evidence is the kind that will scale or the kind that will collapse (Al-Ubaydli et al. 2017; List 2022).</p>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Voltage drop</div><div class="definition-text">The reduction in an intervention&rsquo;s treatment effect as it moves from a controlled pilot to full-scale delivery. A programme that lifts test scores by 0.30 SD in a trial and 0.05 SD at scale has lost five-sixths of its voltage.</div></div>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Spark.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Scaling is not a growth stage; it is a different intervention</div><p>When you scale, you change who receives the programme, who delivers it, and the environment it operates in. The thing running at national scale is not a bigger copy of the pilot &mdash; it is a new intervention that happens to share a name. <strong>The pilot&rsquo;s evidence is evidence about the pilot, not a promise about the scaled version.</strong></p></div>
<h3>The Five Voltage Drops</h3>
<p>List identifies five distinct reasons an effect fails to travel. They are not interchangeable: each has a different early warning sign and a different design-time remedy. A serious scaling decision interrogates all five before committing a budget.</p>
<div class="table-wrapper"><table><thead><tr><th>Voltage drop</th><th>What goes wrong</th><th>Design-time diagnostic</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>1. False positive</td><td>The original result was a statistical fluke &mdash; noise, a small sample, or one lucky draw &mdash; and it will not replicate.</td><td>Has it been <em>independently replicated</em> at least once? Was the sample large and pre-registered? Treat a single significant estimate as a hypothesis, not a finding.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2. Non-representative people</td><td>The pilot sample was not like the population you will scale to &mdash; more motivated, more eligible, more reachable.</td><td>How were participants selected? Would the marginal person added at scale respond as strongly as the average pilot volunteer?</td></tr>
<tr><td>3. Non-representative situation</td><td>The pilot ran under special conditions &mdash; a star NGO team, close researcher oversight, unusual local support &mdash; that scale cannot reproduce.</td><td>Who delivered it, and can the system that will scale it match their skill and motivation? Strip out the researchers &mdash; does the effect survive?</td></tr>
<tr><td>4. Spillovers</td><td>General-equilibrium effects invisible at pilot size appear at scale: market prices shift, services congest, politics reacts.</td><td>If everyone got this at once, what would move &mdash; wages, prices, queues, incentives? Does the mechanism depend on <em>few</em> people having it?</td></tr>
<tr><td>5. Negative economies of scale</td><td>The marginal cost <em>rises</em> as you grow &mdash; harder-to-reach people, thinner supervision, quality control that does not multiply cheaply &mdash; killing cost-effectiveness even if the effect holds.</td><td>Does the per-person cost fall or rise with the next 10,000 people? Is the key ingredient (talent, trust, oversight) cheap to replicate or inherently scarce?</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Drops 2 and 3 are the quiet killers.</strong> They rarely show up as failure in the pilot &mdash; the pilot looks like a triumph. That is exactly why they mislead: the very features that made the trial succeed (eager participants, gifted implementers, intensive oversight) are the features that will not survive scale.</p></div></div>
<h3>The Pilot-to-Scale Collapse Mechanism</h3>
<p>Why do pilots systematically flatter interventions? Because the machinery of a good pilot selects for success in ways that vanish at scale. Participants who enrol in a trial are motivated volunteers, not the reluctant average citizen. Implementers are often hand-picked, trained by the designers, and watched closely. And the delivery agent is frequently the researchers themselves or a high-capacity NGO &mdash; not the line bureaucracy that will actually run the programme.</p>
<div class="callout callout-red"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>The principal&ndash;agent gap.</strong> At scale the designer (principal) hands delivery to a bureaucracy (agent) whose incentives, capacity and attention differ. The agent has other duties, weaker monitoring, and no personal stake in the mechanism firing. The intervention that survives is not the one the pilot tested &mdash; it is whatever the agent can be made to deliver reliably.</p></div></div>
<div class="stat-grid">
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">0.30 &rarr; 0.05</div><div class="stat-label">Illustrative effect (SD) from trial to scale &mdash; a five-sixths voltage drop</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">~50%</div><div class="stat-label">Share of promising results List estimates fail to hold their voltage when scaled</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">5</div><div class="stat-label">Distinct voltage drops &mdash; each with its own diagnostic and remedy</div></div>
</div>
<h3>Case &mdash; NGO vs Government Implementation</h3>
<div class="worked-example"><div class="worked-example-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_User.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Case &mdash; the Kenya contract teachers</div>
<p>An extra contract teacher, hired locally on a short-term contract and accountable to the community, had raised learning in earlier trials. Kenya tested the same intervention two ways at once: delivered by an <strong>NGO</strong>, and delivered by the <strong>government</strong> &mdash; the body that would actually run it at scale (Bold et al. 2018).</p>
<p><strong>The result split cleanly.</strong> Under NGO delivery the contract teachers raised test scores, replicating the earlier finding. Under government delivery the effect was essentially zero. The intervention had not changed &mdash; the implementer had. Political and bureaucratic dynamics (union pressure, delayed contracts, weaker local accountability, contract teachers absorbed into civil-service norms) drained the voltage the moment the state took over.</p>
<p><strong>The design lesson.</strong> This is voltage drop #3 &mdash; a non-representative <em>situation</em> &mdash; made visible because the study deliberately tested delivery by the eventual scaling agent. Duflo, Hanna and Ryan&rsquo;s camera-monitoring of teacher attendance tells a parallel story: it worked crisply under NGO delivery with tight follow-through, and its record under routine government administration has been far more mixed (Duflo et al. 2012). <strong>If you want to know whether an intervention will scale, pilot it under the conditions of scale &mdash; the real implementer, not a proxy.</strong></p></div>
<div class="callout callout-green"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Spark.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p>The remedy is not to abandon promising interventions but to <em>pilot honestly</em>: recruit a representative sample, hand delivery to the actual scaling agent, and price the marginal &mdash; not the average &mdash; unit. An intervention that keeps its voltage under those harsher conditions is one you can scale with confidence (List 2022).</p></div></div>
<div class="reflection-prompt"><p><strong>Reflect.</strong> Take an intervention you believe should be scaled. Walk it through all five voltage drops using the diagnostic column. Which drop is it most exposed to &mdash; and could you redesign the <em>next</em> pilot so that this weakness would show up before, not after, national rollout?</p></div>
<blockquote>&ldquo;The question is not whether an idea works, but whether it will still work when it is no longer yours to run.&rdquo;<cite>&mdash; after John A. List, <em>The Voltage Effect</em> (2022)</cite></blockquote>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Test your grasp of the voltage effect and the five voltage drops before moving to the mechanics of scaling.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> A programme raises test scores by 0.30 SD in a well-run trial but only 0.05 SD once a government runs it nationally. In List&rsquo;s terms, this loss of effect is called: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m11q1" id="m11q1a" value="a"><label for="m11q1a">Elite capture.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m11q1" id="m11q1b" value="b"><label for="m11q1b">Isomorphic mimicry.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m11q1" id="m11q1c" value="c"><label for="m11q1c">A voltage drop &mdash; the effect failing to travel from pilot to scale.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m11q1" id="m11q1d" value="d"><label for="m11q1d">A false positive that never existed in the first place.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> In the Kenya contract-teacher study (Bold et al. 2018), the intervention raised learning under NGO delivery but produced essentially no effect under government delivery. This best illustrates which voltage drop? <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m11q2" id="m11q2a" value="a"><label for="m11q2a">A false positive &mdash; the original result was pure statistical noise.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m11q2" id="m11q2b" value="b"><label for="m11q2b">A non-representative situation &mdash; the pilot&rsquo;s implementer could not be reproduced by the scaling agent.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m11q2" id="m11q2c" value="c"><label for="m11q2c">Negative economies of scale &mdash; the per-person cost rose.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m11q2" id="m11q2d" value="d"><label for="m11q2d">A spillover &mdash; market prices shifted at scale.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="d"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> A designer wants to know in advance whether an intervention will keep its voltage at scale. Which single step most directly guards against the pilot-to-scale collapse? <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m11q3" id="m11q3a" value="a"><label for="m11q3a">Increase the pilot&rsquo;s budget so the effect is larger.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m11q3" id="m11q3b" value="b"><label for="m11q3b">Recruit the most motivated volunteers to maximise the measured effect.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m11q3" id="m11q3c" value="c"><label for="m11q3c">Keep the researchers closely involved in delivery to protect quality.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m11q3" id="m11q3d" value="d"><label for="m11q3d">Pilot under the conditions of scale &mdash; a representative sample and the actual implementer who will run it.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Pick an intervention you would like to scale. Which of the five voltage drops is it most exposed to, and how would you redesign the next pilot so that weakness reveals itself <em>before</em> national rollout rather than after?</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('intervention', 12, 'Module 12: Pathways to Scale',
  'The three real routes from pilot to population — government adoption, NGO replication, and market — what "scaling" actually means, and what survives contact with the bureaucracy.',
  $html$<h2>Module 12: Pathways to Scale</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">A pilot that works is a beginning, not a victory. Between a proven model and a changed population sits a gap that swallows most good interventions &mdash; not because the evidence was wrong, but because nobody chose, deliberately, <em>which road to scale</em> the intervention would travel. There are only three real roads: through the state, through replication by civil society, or through the market. Each buys a different mix of reach, fidelity, and durability, and each exacts a different price. This module is about choosing the road with your eyes open, and about the harder question hiding underneath it &mdash; scaling <em>what</em>, exactly?</p></div>

<h3>Three Roads From Pilot to Population</h3>
<p>Scale is not a single act. It is a choice among delivery channels, made long before the last district is reached. Government adoption offers reach and durability but dilutes the model as it passes through the bureaucracy. NGO replication protects fidelity but rarely touches population scale. Market delivery is self-sustaining where people can pay and blind where they cannot. Most designers back into one of these by default; the discipline is to pick on purpose.</p>
<div class="comparison-cards">
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_User.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></span><span class="comparison-title">Government adoption</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p><strong>Reach:</strong> population-scale, potentially universal.</p><p><strong>Fidelity:</strong> low &mdash; the model is diluted by civil-service incentives.</p><p><strong>Durability:</strong> high &mdash; it lives on a budget line after you leave.</p><p><strong>Cost per head:</strong> lowest, using existing staff and systems.</p></div></div>
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Layers.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></span><span class="comparison-title">NGO replication</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p><strong>Reach:</strong> bounded by your own fundraising and management span.</p><p><strong>Fidelity:</strong> high &mdash; you control training, supervision, and culture.</p><p><strong>Durability:</strong> tied to grant cycles; ends when funding does.</p><p><strong>Cost per head:</strong> high &mdash; you pay for the whole delivery machine.</p></div></div>
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Arrow_upward.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></span><span class="comparison-title">Market / commercial</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p><strong>Reach:</strong> self-scaling wherever demand and ability-to-pay exist.</p><p><strong>Fidelity:</strong> shaped by price competition, not by your design.</p><p><strong>Durability:</strong> high while the business is viable.</p><p><strong>Cost per head:</strong> borne by the user &mdash; which excludes the poorest.</p></div></div>
</div>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p>No road gives you reach, fidelity, and durability at once. Choosing government buys reach and durability at the cost of fidelity; choosing an NGO buys fidelity at the cost of reach; choosing the market buys durability at the cost of the poorest. The pathway <em>is</em> a trade-off, and pretending otherwise is how programmes drift into a channel that cannot carry them.</p></div></div>

<h3>Scaling What? The Question Beneath the Question</h3>
<p>Before choosing a road, decide what actually travels. "Scale the programme" is usually the wrong instruction. What replicates well is often the <em>principle</em> behind the programme, not its exact form &mdash; a lesson learned painfully whenever a lovingly detailed model is copied and produces nothing (the isomorphic mimicry of Module 1). Distinguish three verbs and three objects.</p>
<div class="table-wrapper"><table><thead><tr><th>You can scale&hellip;</th><th>What travels</th><th>When it is the right target</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><strong>The organisation</strong></td><td>Your team, brand, and delivery machine grow to new places.</td><td>When the result depends on your specific culture and supervision, and quality cannot be codified.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>The model</strong></td><td>The full package &mdash; protocol, materials, staffing &mdash; is handed to others to run.</td><td>When the package is well-specified and its parts are the active ingredient.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>The principle</strong></td><td>The insight (e.g. "teach to the child's level, not the grade") diffuses into how others already work.</td><td>When the mechanism is robust but the exact form must bend to each system.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Three directions of travel cut across these. <em>Scaling-up</em> moves the intervention vertically into policy and institutions &mdash; a budget line, a national guideline. <em>Scaling-out</em> replicates it horizontally to more people and places. <em>Scaling-deep</em> shifts the norms and relationships that hold a problem in place, so change outlasts any single programme (the Waterloo scaling typology, McConnell 2017). A serious scaling plan usually needs all three, not one.</p>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Spark.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Scale the insight, not the artefact</div><p>The interventions that reach millions rarely do so by cloning the pilot. They isolate the one mechanism that carries the effect, strip away the scaffolding the pilot needed, and let host systems rebuild the rest in their own idiom. <strong>Ask of every element: is this the active ingredient, or the packaging?</strong> Only the active ingredient must travel intact.</p></div>

<h3>The Handoff to Government</h3>
<p>The state is the only actor that can reach every child, every farmer, every village &mdash; so most durable scale runs through it eventually. But a lean pilot built on hand-picked, highly motivated staff must survive contact with a system that runs on transfers, procurement rules, seniority, and a thousand competing mandates. What survives the handoff is what has been designed to survive it.</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Crosshair_detailed.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> What survives contact with the bureaucracy</div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Incentive fit</div><div class="definition-text">Tasks that a transferred, overstretched government teacher or ANM can actually do within existing duties survive; tasks that assume a dedicated, mission-driven cadre do not.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Procurement &amp; staffing reality</div><div class="definition-text">If the model needs materials the system cannot procure or posts it cannot sanction, it dies quietly at the first budget cycle.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Simplicity of the protocol</div><div class="definition-text">A one-page instruction that a monitoring officer can verify scales; a rich but tacit practice that lived in the trainers&rsquo; heads evaporates.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">A political owner</div><div class="definition-text">Without a secretary or minister whose name is on the result, the reform has no defender when priorities shift.</div></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-blue"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Info.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p>The <strong>embedded-partner model</strong> has become the dominant route for evidence organisations in South Asia: rather than delivering at scale themselves, they sit <em>inside</em> government. J-PAL and IPA run state cells that co-design and evaluate reforms; Pratham partners with state education departments to train and mentor government teachers. The NGO supplies rigour and the missing capability; the state supplies reach, staff, and permanence. The intervention scales as the government&rsquo;s own programme, not the NGO&rsquo;s (Kremer, Gugerty 2018 J-PAL scaling).</p></div></div>

<h3>Adaptive Scaling: Iterate, Don&rsquo;t Freeze</h3>
<p>The instinct at scale is to lock the model &mdash; write the manual, forbid deviation, protect fidelity. But an intervention that worked in three districts meets genuinely new constraints in the next three hundred, and a frozen blueprint cannot answer them. Problem-Driven Iterative Adaptation (Andrews, Pritchett &amp; Woolcock 2017) argues the opposite: treat scaling as continued problem-solving, adapting the model to each context through fast cycles of trying, learning, and adjusting, while holding the core mechanism fixed. Fidelity to the <em>mechanism</em>, flexibility on the <em>form</em>.</p>
<div class="callout callout-purple"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Target.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p>Scale is not a single leap but a staircase, and <strong>the evidence does not travel with you up the stairs</strong>. An effect measured at 5,000 households can shrink, vanish, or reverse at 500,000 &mdash; delivery quality falls, the marginal participant is less motivated, general-equilibrium effects kick in. Scaling-readiness frameworks (3ie; J-PAL) therefore insist on <em>re-testing at each step</em>: pilot, then a larger at-scale trial under real delivery conditions, then monitored rollout &mdash; not one small RCT stretched to justify a national programme (bridge to Module 13).</p></div></div>

<div class="worked-example"><div class="worked-example-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Clipboard.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Case &mdash; government adoption vs NGO replication</div>
<p>The intervention: Teaching at the Right Level (TaRL), the Pratham model that regroups children by learning level for a focused daily block and produces large, replicated reading and arithmetic gains.</p>
<p><strong>Road one &mdash; NGO delivery.</strong> Pratham runs TaRL directly with its own volunteers and staff. Fidelity is superb and the results are clean, but reach is capped at what Pratham can fundraise and manage, and delivery ends where the grant ends. High fidelity, bounded reach, fragile durability.</p>
<p><strong>Road two &mdash; government adoption.</strong> In Haryana&rsquo;s <em>Mission Buniyaad</em>, the model was handed to the state to run through its own government teachers across thousands of schools. Reach multiplied enormously and it sat on the state&rsquo;s budget. But fidelity had to be defended constantly: government teachers had competing duties, the daily level-based block was the first thing squeezed, and gains held only where mentoring and political ownership held. Population reach and durability, bought with a permanent fight over fidelity.</p>
<p><strong>The lesson.</strong> Neither road is "correct." Pratham keeps both alive on purpose &mdash; direct delivery to prove and refine the model, government partnership to reach the population &mdash; and what it hands the state is the <em>principle</em> (teach to level) plus the minimum protocol to enact it, not its full organisational apparatus. The pathway choice was a design decision, made deliberately, not a default. Module 13 takes up how to keep the mechanism alive once the state owns it.</p></div>

<div class="reflection-prompt"><p><strong>Reflect.</strong> Take an intervention you would like to see reach a whole population. Which of the three roads &mdash; government, NGO replication, market &mdash; can actually carry it, given who it serves and who can pay? Now name what would travel down that road: the organisation, the model, or just the principle? What is the one active ingredient that must survive intact, and what is packaging you can let each host system rebuild?</p></div>

<blockquote>&ldquo;The goal is not to scale a project, but to scale a solution &mdash; and often the solution is an idea that others must be free to make their own.&rdquo;<cite>&mdash; after Lant Pritchett, Michael Woolcock &amp; Matt Andrews</cite></blockquote>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Test your grasp of the three pathways to scale before moving to institutionalisation in Module 13.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> A designer wants a proven literacy intervention to reach every child in a state and to survive after the funding ends, and accepts that the model will be simplified in the process. Which pathway best fits these priorities? <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m12q1" id="m12q1a" value="a"><label for="m12q1a">Market delivery, so users pay for the service and it self-scales.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m12q1" id="m12q1b" value="b"><label for="m12q1b">NGO replication, because it protects the fidelity of the model.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m12q1" id="m12q1c" value="c"><label for="m12q1c">Government adoption, which trades fidelity for population reach and durability.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m12q1" id="m12q1d" value="d"><label for="m12q1d">Keep the pilot small and run more RCTs before doing anything.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> "Scale the principle, not the artefact" advises a designer to: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m12q2" id="m12q2a" value="a"><label for="m12q2a">Copy the pilot&rsquo;s exact protocol, materials, and staffing into every new site.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m12q2" id="m12q2b" value="b"><label for="m12q2b">Identify the active mechanism and let host systems rebuild the surrounding form in their own way.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m12q2" id="m12q2c" value="c"><label for="m12q2c">Grow the delivery organisation and brand into every new district.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m12q2" id="m12q2d" value="d"><label for="m12q2d">Freeze the model as a fixed blueprint and forbid any local deviation.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="d"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> According to adaptive scaling and scaling-readiness thinking (PDIA; J-PAL/3ie), why should an intervention be re-tested at each step up in scale? <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m12q3" id="m12q3a" value="a"><label for="m12q3a">Because donors require a new report at every funding round.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m12q3" id="m12q3b" value="b"><label for="m12q3b">Because a larger sample always produces a larger and more reliable effect.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m12q3" id="m12q3c" value="c"><label for="m12q3c">Because the pilot&rsquo;s exact blueprint should be enforced more strictly at scale.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m12q3" id="m12q3d" value="d"><label for="m12q3d">Because the effect measured in a pilot can shrink, vanish, or reverse under real at-scale delivery conditions.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Take an intervention you would like to scale. Which of the three roads can actually carry it given who it serves and who can pay &mdash; and at which step up in scale is its measured effect most likely to fade?</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('intervention', 13, 'Module 13: Designing for Adaptation & Learning',
  'Blueprint versus process design, adaptive management and Problem-Driven Iterative Adaptation, and building learning loops into the intervention itself rather than bolting them on.',
  $html$<h2>Module 13: Designing for Adaptation &amp; Learning</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">Most of this course has treated design as something you finish before implementation begins &mdash; diagnose, theorise, choose a model, then deliver. But some interventions face problems that cannot be fully specified in advance, in contexts that vary faster than any blueprint can anticipate. For these, the design decision is not <em>what to build</em> but <em>how to keep learning</em> &mdash; how to build an intervention that generates the evidence to improve itself while it runs. This module is about designing the learning loop into the intervention, not bolting evaluation on at the end.</p></div>
<h3>Blueprint vs Process Design</h3>
<p>David Korten&rsquo;s 1980 study of rural development in Asia drew a distinction that has organised the field ever since. A <em>blueprint</em> approach fixes the design up front, writes it into a manual, and rolls it out uniformly &mdash; the plan is the master, and deviation is failure. A <em>learning process</em> approach treats the early intervention as a question, not an answer: it starts small, fits the programme to the local reality, and lets the design emerge from what the fit teaches. Korten&rsquo;s insight was that the successful programmes he studied &mdash; unlike the blueprints that failed &mdash; had all been through a learning process first (Korten 1980).</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Layers.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Korten&rsquo;s three stages of the learning process</div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Learning to be effective</div><div class="definition-text">The programme is small and often fails, but errors are embraced as information. The question is whether the mechanism fires at all in this context.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Learning to be efficient</div><div class="definition-text">Once it works, strip out what is not essential. Reduce the cost and the staff intensity so the model can survive beyond a hand-picked team.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Learning to expand</div><div class="definition-text">Only now does the organisation build the systems and capacities to scale &mdash; because it finally knows what it is scaling.</div></div></div>
<p>The choice between the two is not ideological &mdash; it is a match to the problem. When the problem is well understood and the context is uniform, a blueprint is cheaper, faster, and fairer. When the problem is complex and the context varies, a blueprint locks in the wrong answer at scale.</p>
<div class="comparison-cards">
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Clipboard.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></span><span class="comparison-title">Blueprint design</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p>Fits well-understood problems in uniform contexts &mdash; a vaccination cold chain, a cash transfer with fixed eligibility.</p><p>Standardises: one manual, one protocol, executed the same everywhere.</p><p>Optimises for fidelity, cost control, and equity of treatment.</p><p>Fails when the context varies or the mechanism is not yet known &mdash; it scales the wrong design confidently.</p></div></div>
<div class="comparison-card"><div class="comparison-header"><span class="comparison-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Settings.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></span><span class="comparison-title">Process design</span></div><div class="comparison-list"><p>Fits complex problems in variable contexts &mdash; governance reform, behaviour change, frontline service quality.</p><p>Standardises the <em>learning method</em>, not the activity; the local design is allowed to differ.</p><p>Optimises for fit, discovery, and speed of correction.</p><p>Fails when abused as an excuse for no plan, no measurement, and no accountability.</p></div></div>
</div>
<h3>Adaptive Management and PDIA</h3>
<p>The Problem-Driven Iterative Adaptation approach gives the learning process a discipline (Andrews, Pritchett &amp; Woolcock 2017). Rather than importing a best-practice solution and forcing local reality to comply &mdash; the isomorphic mimicry of Module 1 &mdash; PDIA starts from a locally-felt problem and builds the solution through many small, monitored iterations. Each step is a cheap experiment: try something, watch closely, keep what works, drop what does not, and let the next step be shaped by the last. The authors call this <em>crawling the design space</em> &mdash; you do not know the route in advance, so you feel your way toward it, one short, reversible move at a time.</p>
<div class="callout callout-blue"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Info.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Positive deviance</strong> is the adaptive designer&rsquo;s favourite raw material. In almost any programme, a few sites, workers, or households already get better results than the rest with the same resources. Instead of importing an outside model, find these deviants, learn what they do differently, and design the next iteration around their practice. It is discovery from inside the system rather than prescription from outside it.</p></div></div>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Spark.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Iterations must be small, fast, and monitored</div><p>Adaptation is not the freedom to change anything at any time. Its power comes from a tight loop: a change small enough that its effect can be attributed, fast enough that you learn before the window closes, and monitored closely enough that you actually see the effect. An unmonitored change is not adaptation &mdash; it is drift. <strong>The learning loop, not the flexibility, is the design.</strong></p></div>
<h3>Developmental Evaluation and Rapid-Cycle Testing</h3>
<p>The evaluation traditions of the MEL and Causal Inference flagships answer a summative question &mdash; <em>did it work?</em> &mdash; and answer it rigorously, at the end. Michael Quinn Patton&rsquo;s <em>developmental evaluation</em> answers a different question in real time: <em>what should we change next?</em> (Patton 2011). The evaluator sits inside the design team, feeds back observations as the intervention unfolds, and helps the programme adapt while it is still adapting. It is not a softer evaluation &mdash; it is evaluation repurposed from verdict to steering wheel.</p>
<div class="table-wrapper"><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Summative evaluation</th><th>Developmental evaluation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>When</td><td>At the end (or midline and endline)</td><td>Continuously, in fast cycles</td></tr>
<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Judge merit, worth, whether to continue or scale</td><td>Inform the next design decision</td></tr>
<tr><td>Design stability</td><td>Assumes a stable intervention to measure</td><td>Assumes the intervention is still changing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rigour standard</td><td>Attribution &mdash; hand off to the MEL flagship</td><td>Timeliness and usefulness to the decision</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The practical form this takes is <em>rapid-cycle testing</em>: pose a design question, run a small test, read a fast indicator, decide, repeat &mdash; often in weeks rather than the years a summative trial needs. The two are complements, not rivals. Developmental evaluation shapes the intervention until it stabilises; summative evaluation then tests the stabilised model for the rigorous attribution that scaling decisions require.</p>
<h3>Building the Learning Loop Into the Design</h3>
<p>An adaptive intervention is one whose own operation produces the data that improves it. This is a design property, not a reporting afterthought: the indicators, the cadence, and the decision rights all have to be built in from the start. The test of a learning loop is simple &mdash; every piece of data collected must be attached to a decision someone can actually make.</p>
<div class="table-wrapper"><table><thead><tr><th>Learning-loop component</th><th>Design decision it forces</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Decision-focused indicators</td><td>Which few measures would actually change what a frontline worker or manager does next week? Drop indicators no decision depends on.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Feedback cadence</td><td>How fast must data return to be useful &mdash; daily, weekly, monthly? The cadence must beat the pace of the decision it feeds.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Who acts on what</td><td>For each signal, name the actor with the authority and the incentive to respond. A loop with no one at the acting end is just monitoring.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Safe-to-fail space</td><td>Where is deviation permitted and reversible, and where must fidelity hold? Adaptation needs a bounded arena, not the whole programme.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="worked-example"><div class="worked-example-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Arrow_upward.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Case &mdash; an adaptive, iterated programme in South Asia</div>
<p>The problem: children in early grades across a state were enrolled but not learning to read, and past blueprint fixes &mdash; more textbooks, more training days &mdash; had left the gap untouched.</p>
<p><strong>The process design.</strong> Rather than roll out a fixed manual, Pratham&rsquo;s Teaching at the Right Level work moved through Korten&rsquo;s stages: early years learning whether grouping children by level fired at all, then stripping the model down until ordinary government teachers could run it, and only then building the systems to expand. Each state adaptation was a fresh iteration, not a copy.</p>
<p><strong>The learning loop.</strong> A one-page assessment that a teacher administers in minutes doubles as the intervention&rsquo;s core indicator: it tells the teacher which group each child belongs in <em>today</em> (the frontline decision) and, aggregated, tells managers whether the mechanism is firing (the steering decision). The same act of measuring both delivers the intervention and generates the data that improves it &mdash; a loop built into the design, not reported upward after the fact.</p>
<p><strong>The hand-off.</strong> Once the model stabilised, summative trials tested it for rigorous attribution &mdash; the question the MEL flagship equips you to answer. Developmental evaluation shaped the design; summative evaluation validated it for scale.</p></div>
<h3>Data Systems for Delivery, Not Just Reporting</h3>
<p>Modules 6 and 9 warned that a management information system built only to fill the funder&rsquo;s dashboard becomes a tax on frontline time that returns nothing to the people paying it. The adaptive lens sharpens the point: an MIS is part of the intervention&rsquo;s design, and it should be built for the frontline decision first and the upward report second. If the ASHA, the teacher, or the block officer cannot see something in the data that changes what they do this week, the system is extracting information rather than creating it.</p>
<div class="callout callout-amber"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p>The failure signature is familiar across South Asian programmes: dozens of registers and app screens flow upward to a district dashboard, while the worker who collected every number gets nothing back that helps them serve the next household. Data that only travels up is a cost centre. Data that also travels back down &mdash; fast, legible, and tied to a decision the worker owns &mdash; is what turns an MIS into a learning loop.</p></div></div>
<div class="stat-grid">
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">3</div><div class="stat-label">Korten&rsquo;s stages &mdash; effective, efficient, expandable &mdash; before a design is ready to scale</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">Weeks</div><div class="stat-label">The cadence of rapid-cycle testing, against the years a summative trial needs</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-value">1</div><div class="stat-label">Rule: every indicator must attach to a decision someone can act on</div></div>
</div>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Layers.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Hand-off to the MEL flagship</div><p>This module designs the <em>learning system</em> inside an intervention &mdash; which signals, how fast, to whom. It deliberately stops short of measurement rigour. For sampling, indicator validity, attribution, and the machinery of summative and impact evaluation, the ImpactMojo <strong>MEL</strong> and <strong>Causal Inference</strong> flagships are the companion courses. Adaptive design decides <em>what to learn and when to act</em>; those courses decide <em>whether you can believe the number</em>.</p></div>
<div class="reflection-prompt"><p><strong>Reflect.</strong> Take one intervention you know. Is it better served by a blueprint or a learning-process design &mdash; and is it actually being run that way? Now trace one learning loop inside it: name a single decision-focused indicator, its feedback cadence, and the specific person who is supposed to act on it. If any of the three is missing, the loop is broken &mdash; which one, and what would it take to close it?</p></div>
<blockquote>&ldquo;The organisation must embrace error, plan with the people, and link knowledge building with action &mdash; the programme is not the plan; it is what is learned in doing.&rdquo;<cite>&mdash; after David Korten, Community Organization and Rural Development (1980)</cite></blockquote>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">Test your grasp of adaptive, learning-oriented design before moving on.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> According to Korten&rsquo;s distinction, when is a <em>blueprint</em> design the better match rather than a <em>learning process</em> design? <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m13q1" id="m13q1a" value="a"><label for="m13q1a">When the problem is complex and the local context varies widely from site to site.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m13q1" id="m13q1b" value="b"><label for="m13q1b">When the problem is well understood and the context is uniform, so standardisation is cheaper and fairer.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m13q1" id="m13q1c" value="c"><label for="m13q1c">Whenever the funder requires an upward-reporting dashboard.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m13q1" id="m13q1d" value="d"><label for="m13q1d">Blueprint design is always superior because it controls cost.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> In PDIA, what distinguishes genuine adaptive management from mere drift? <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m13q2" id="m13q2a" value="a"><label for="m13q2a">The freedom to change any part of the programme at any time.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m13q2" id="m13q2b" value="b"><label for="m13q2b">Importing a proven best-practice model and enforcing it faithfully.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m13q2" id="m13q2c" value="c"><label for="m13q2c">Iterations that are small, fast, and closely monitored, so each change&rsquo;s effect can be seen and attributed.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m13q2" id="m13q2d" value="d"><label for="m13q2d">Waiting for a summative endline evaluation before changing anything.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> A programme collects dozens of indicators that flow up to a district dashboard, but the ASHA who records them gets nothing back that changes what she does next week. The core design flaw is: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m13q3" id="m13q3a" value="a"><label for="m13q3a">The MIS is built for upward reporting, not for the frontline decision &mdash; the learning loop has no one acting at its end.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m13q3" id="m13q3b" value="b"><label for="m13q3b">Too few indicators are being collected; more registers would help.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m13q3" id="m13q3c" value="c"><label for="m13q3c">The programme should abandon measurement and rely on intuition.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m13q3" id="m13q3d" value="d"><label for="m13q3d">Developmental evaluation should be replaced with a summative trial.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Take one intervention you have worked on. Name a single decision-focused indicator it could collect, the cadence at which that data would need to return, and the specific person who would act on it. Is that loop currently built into the design, or bolted on as a report at the end?</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false),
('intervention', 14, 'Module 14: Design Studio — Your Intervention, End to End',
  'The capstone: carrying one problem through diagnosis, theory of change, model choice, cost-effectiveness, and scale pathway — and confronting the ethics of intervening in other people''s lives.',
  $html$<h2>Module 14: Design Studio &mdash; Your Intervention, End to End</h2>
<div class="section-intro"><p class="lead">Every module so far handed you one instrument &mdash; a diagnosis, a theory of change, a model, a delivery map, a scale test. This capstone is where you assemble them. Pick <strong>one</strong> problem you care about &mdash; a real one, in a district or community you know &mdash; and carry it through the whole pipeline until you can hand a critic a design memo that survives their hardest questions. The test of learning here is not recall; it is whether you can design an intervention that a hostile reviewer, a sceptical funder, and the people it is meant to serve would each recognise as honest.</p></div>
<h3>The End-to-End Design Walk</h3>
<p>An intervention is designed in a sequence, and each step constrains the next. Work them in order &mdash; the discipline is refusing to name a model before you have diagnosed the problem, and refusing to talk scale before you know the mechanism fires. Take your one problem down this ladder.</p>
<div class="capstone-timeline">
<div class="capstone-phase"><div class="phase-number">1</div><div class="phase-content"><h5>Diagnose the problem</h5><p>What kind of problem is this &mdash; information, incentive, capacity, belief, or coordination? Name the binding constraint, not the symptom. (Module 2)</p></div></div>
<div class="capstone-phase"><div class="phase-number">2</div><div class="phase-content"><h5>Write a falsifiable theory of change</h5><p>State the mechanism in one sentence and list the assumptions it rides on. Mark the one assumption that, if wrong, sinks the whole design. (Module 3)</p></div></div>
<div class="capstone-phase"><div class="phase-number">3</div><div class="phase-content"><h5>Choose and adapt a model</h5><p>Cash, a bundle, a frontline cadre, a collective, a service redesign &mdash; select the model whose logic matches your mechanism, then adapt it to local reality rather than copying its form. (Modules 4&ndash;8)</p></div></div>
<div class="capstone-phase"><div class="phase-number">4</div><div class="phase-content"><h5>Map the delivery chain</h5><p>Trace every link from funder to last mile. Find the weakest link before the field does. (Module 9)</p></div></div>
<div class="capstone-phase"><div class="phase-number">5</div><div class="phase-content"><h5>Check incentives, targeting, cost-effectiveness</h5><p>Are the frontline incentives compatible with the goal? Does the benefit reach the intended group? Is the cost per outcome defensible against the next-best use of the money? (Module 10)</p></div></div>
<div class="capstone-phase"><div class="phase-number">6</div><div class="phase-content"><h5>Plan the scale pathway and voltage checks</h5><p>Name who scales it &mdash; state, market, or NGO federation &mdash; and where you expect voltage to drop as motivated pilot staff give way to an ordinary system. (Modules 11&ndash;12)</p></div></div>
<div class="capstone-phase"><div class="phase-number">7</div><div class="phase-content"><h5>Build in learning loops</h5><p>Decide in advance what evidence would make you change course, and how it reaches you fast enough to act. A design with no learning loop cannot be corrected. (Module 13)</p></div></div>
</div>
<div class="key-insight"><div class="key-insight-label"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Spark.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Design is a chain of commitments, not a menu</div><p>You cannot skip a rung and patch it later cheaply. A weak diagnosis is not rescued by a clever model; a broken delivery chain is not rescued by a bigger budget; a missing learning loop is not rescued at scale. <strong>Each step you finish honestly makes the next one narrower and safer.</strong> The memo at the end of this module is simply this ladder, written down so someone else can attack it.</p></div>
<h3>The Design Critique Rubric</h3>
<p>Before you defend a design, attack it. Each failure mode this course named has a single diagnostic question a good critic asks first. Run your own intervention through every row &mdash; if you cannot answer a question with evidence, that is where your design is thin.</p>
<div class="table-wrapper"><table><thead><tr><th>Failure mode</th><th>The critic&rsquo;s diagnostic question</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Solution in search of a problem</td><td>What is the binding constraint, and would this design survive if the problem turned out to be a different one?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Isomorphic mimicry</td><td>Does this reproduce the <em>function</em> of the model you copied, or only its visible form &mdash; the committee, the plan, the app?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Broken delivery chain</td><td>Which single link is weakest, and what happens to the outcome if it leaks by 30 percent?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pilot-to-scale collapse</td><td>What in your pilot depended on hand-picked staff or motivated volunteers that an ordinary system will not have?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Elite capture</td><td>Who is powerful enough near the point of delivery to divert the benefit, and what stops them?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Incentive incompatibility</td><td>What is the frontline worker actually rewarded and punished for &mdash; and does that pull toward or against the goal?</td></tr>
<tr><td>No learning loop</td><td>What evidence would tell you this is failing, how fast would it reach you, and are you committed to act on it?</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h3>The Ethics of Intervention</h3>
<p>An intervention is an exercise of power over other people&rsquo;s lives, usually people with less power than the designer. Competence at design does not confer the right to intervene; that right is earned through restraint, consent, and humility about whose problem is being solved.</p>
<div class="concept-box"><div class="concept-box-header"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_User.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"> Six obligations you carry into the field</div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Do no harm</div><div class="definition-text">Ask what the intervention could break &mdash; local markets, social trust, existing coping strategies &mdash; not only what it might build.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Dignity and agency</div><div class="definition-text">Treat participants as authors of their own lives, not as units to be moved. A design that works by removing choice has usually solved the wrong problem.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Informed consent</div><div class="definition-text">People should understand what is being done, by whom, and why &mdash; in their language, before they are enrolled, not buried in a form.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">The right to exit</div><div class="definition-text">Participation must be refusable without penalty. An intervention no one can leave is a form of coercion, however good its intentions.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">Guard against paternalism</div><div class="definition-text">&ldquo;We know what is good for them&rdquo; is the sentence that precedes most avoidable harm. Design with people, not merely for them.</div></div>
<div class="definition"><div class="definition-term">The politics of helping</div><div class="definition-text">Whose problem is being solved &mdash; the community&rsquo;s, the funder&rsquo;s, or the designer&rsquo;s? Who decided it was a problem, and who was in the room?</div></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-red"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Alert.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Power and humility.</strong> The people you design for have survived without you and will remain after you leave. They understand their own constraints better than any diagnostic can. If your design assumes they are ignorant, lazy, or irrational, the fault is almost always in the design, not the people. Across South Asia the interventions that endured &mdash; SHG federations, the ASHA cadre, community-managed water &mdash; succeeded because they routed decisions <em>through</em> the community rather than around it. Ask before every rollout: would the intended participants, if they had full power to choose, choose this?</p></div></div>
<h3>The Deliverable &mdash; A One-Intervention Design Memo</h3>
<p>Your capstone output is a single memo a practitioner could hand to a funder or a government partner. Nine fields, each answerable in a few sentences. If a field is empty, that step of the design is not finished. Fill it for your one problem.</p>
<div class="table-wrapper"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Field</th><th>The one thing it must state</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Problem</td><td>The binding constraint, for whom, backed by one piece of evidence &mdash; not the symptom.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Theory of change</td><td>The mechanism in one sentence, and the assumption that would sink it if wrong.</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Model</td><td>Which model you chose and the local adaptation that keeps its function, not just its form.</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Targeting</td><td>Who is in, who is out, and how you keep the benefit from the already-powerful.</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Delivery chain</td><td>The links from funder to last mile, and the weakest one named honestly.</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>Cost-effectiveness</td><td>Cost per outcome, and why it beats the next-best use of the same money.</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>Scale pathway</td><td>Who scales it and where you expect voltage to drop.</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>Learning plan</td><td>The evidence that would make you change course, and how fast it reaches you.</td></tr>
<tr><td>9</td><td>Biggest risk</td><td>The single thing most likely to make this fail &mdash; stated plainly, not buried.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="callout callout-green"><div class="callout-icon"><img src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/sargam-icons@1.6.6/Icons/Line/si_Flag.svg" alt="" class="sargam-icon"></div><div class="callout-content"><p><strong>Field 9 is the one that matters most.</strong> A memo that names its biggest risk plainly is more trustworthy than one that hides it. A designer who cannot state how their intervention might fail has not finished thinking &mdash; and a funder who never hears the risk cannot help you manage it. Write the risk you are most tempted to leave out.</p></div></div>
<div class="reflection-prompt"><p><strong>Studio task.</strong> Take the one problem you chose at the start. Walk it down all seven steps, run it through every row of the critique rubric, and write the nine-field memo. Then give the memo to someone who disagrees with you and ask them to find the row where your evidence is thinnest. Rewrite that row. That is the whole discipline of this course in one afternoon.</p></div>
<blockquote>&ldquo;The people who design an intervention are rarely the people who must live inside it. Humility is not a virtue added at the end &mdash; it is a design constraint from the first line.&rdquo;<cite>&mdash; adapted from the practice of community-led development in South Asia</cite></blockquote>$html$,
  $quiz$<div class="quiz-section"><div class="quiz-header"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"/></svg><h4>Check Your Understanding</h4></div><p class="quiz-intro">This capstone quiz tests the integrative design logic &mdash; sequencing the steps, choosing a scale pathway, and spotting an ethics failure.</p><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="b"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">1</span> In the end-to-end design walk, why must diagnosis come before choosing a model? <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m14q1" id="m14q1a" value="a"><label for="m14q1a">Because funders require a diagnosis section before releasing money.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m14q1" id="m14q1b" value="b"><label for="m14q1b">Because each step constrains the next &mdash; a model chosen before the binding constraint is known is a solution in search of a problem.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m14q1" id="m14q1c" value="c"><label for="m14q1c">Because diagnosis is cheaper to run than a pilot.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m14q1" id="m14q1d" value="d"><label for="m14q1d">Because models cannot be adapted once a problem is defined.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="c"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">2</span> A pilot works beautifully with a hand-picked, highly motivated field team. When planning the scale pathway, the critique rubric asks you to identify: <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m14q2" id="m14q2a" value="a"><label for="m14q2a">How much more funding the national rollout will need.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m14q2" id="m14q2b" value="b"><label for="m14q2b">Which awards the pilot team should receive for their results.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m14q2" id="m14q2c" value="c"><label for="m14q2c">What in the pilot depended on that special team, which an ordinary system will not have &mdash; the likely source of voltage drop.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m14q2" id="m14q2d" value="d"><label for="m14q2d">Whether the pilot should simply be repeated identically in every district.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="quiz-question" data-correct="a"><div class="question-text"><span class="question-number">3</span> A designer enrolls a village in a livelihoods programme without explaining it in the local language and makes participation a condition for receiving an unrelated government benefit. Which ethics obligations are being violated? <span class="question-type">Multiple Choice</span></div><div class="quiz-options"><div class="quiz-option" data-option="a"><input type="radio" name="m14q3" id="m14q3a" value="a"><label for="m14q3a">Informed consent and the right to exit.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="b"><input type="radio" name="m14q3" id="m14q3b" value="b"><label for="m14q3b">Cost-effectiveness and targeting.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="c"><input type="radio" name="m14q3" id="m14q3c" value="c"><label for="m14q3c">Isomorphic mimicry and elite capture.</label></div><div class="quiz-option" data-option="d"><input type="radio" name="m14q3" id="m14q3d" value="d"><label for="m14q3d">No obligation is violated if the programme ultimately helps them.</label></div></div><button class="check-answer-btn" onclick="checkAnswer(this)"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg> Check Answer</button><div class="quiz-feedback"></div></div><div class="reflection-prompt"><p>Return to the nine-field design memo for your own intervention. Which field is currently the emptiest &mdash; and which failure mode from the rubric does that emptiness expose you to?</p></div></div>$quiz$,
  false);
